Revision history for File-SOPS

{{$NEXT}}
  - The order-preserving reparse that gives MAC verification its key order is
    now asked of the FORMAT HANDLER instead of being a YAML::PP call inside
    File::SOPS. Both shipped handlers gained a documented
    `parse_in_document_order` class method; `File::SOPS::Format::JSON`
    delegates to `File::SOPS::Format::YAML`'s, which is what already happened
    and is now stated where it can be revisited for one format alone. Nothing
    a caller does changes and NO DIGEST MOVES: measured over 63 encrypted
    documents - 21 written by sops 3.13.3, 42 by this library, 17 of them
    order-sensitive - every one recovered document order before and after and
    every digest is identical. The point is the formats that do not exist yet:
    YAML::PP cannot read an env or ini file at all, so verification for those
    would have fallen back to sorted key order in silence, and an env file
    sops writes is in DOCUMENT order. Every such file whose keys were not
    already sorted would have failed its MAC with nothing pointing at the
    cause. A format class that cannot recover order at all is now a loud
    error rather than a silent fallback - that is a hole in the distribution,
    not in the file. See docs/adr/0036, which refines docs/adr/0001 rather
    than changing it, and karr #74 (prerequisite for the ENV and INI handlers,
    karr #36 and #37).
  - BUG FIX and BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: `edit` can now save a YAML document that
    holds a bare `.inf`, `-.inf` or `.nan` in an unencrypted slot, and so can
    `encrypt_file` and `encrypt_in_place`. Before this, opening such a
    document - one `sops -e` writes by default from a plaintext `.inf` -
    changing a completely unrelated key and saving died with the untouched
    leaf refused, AND DESTROYED THE EDIT: the temporary file the editor wrote
    to is already gone by the time the re-encryption runs, so the only copy of
    what was typed is whatever the editor still has in its buffer. `sops edit`
    on the same three documents is exit 0 with the wire unchanged, measured
    against sops 3.13.3. The cause was one gate: since 0.003 a YAML scalar the
    document wrote PLAIN, whose token go-yaml resolves to a non-finite float,
    comes back as that float carrying the document's own token - but only for
    a document that carried a `sops:` section. `edit` hands the editor a
    plaintext, so the leaf came back as the string `.inf` and the emitter's
    foreign-resolution guard correctly refused to write it. The same gate meant
    this library's own `decrypt_file` wrote `v_unencrypted: .inf` and its own
    `encrypt_file` then refused to read that file back. sops makes one parse
    and now so does this: the repair runs on every document, plaintext
    included. Measured, twelve spellings x two slots on plaintext input: the
    twelve unencrypted rows go from 0 written to 12 written with `sops -d`
    exit 0, and for the three spellings `sops -e` itself writes the wire is
    byte-identical to sops's. A QUOTED `".inf"` is still the string it always
    was, and so are the near misses (`.INf`, `Inf`, `.infinity`), `config.info`
    and `1e400` - the repair asks YAML::PP how the document WROTE the scalar
    and never matches the leaf's text. JSON is untouched in both directions.
    The deliberate cost: a bare token under a key that gets ENCRYPTED is now
    refused, naming the key path, where it used to be written as a `type:str`
    holding `.inf` - a working file whose value had silently stopped being a
    number, since `sops -e` writes a `type:float` there. That refusal goes
    away with karr #122. Still open and now documented on `edit`: an ENCRYPTED
    non-finite float is silently retyped to a string by an edit, because its
    only plaintext spelling from this emitter is a bare `Inf`, which go-yaml
    reads as a string (karr #134). See docs/adr/0034 and karr #123.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and BUG FIX: a YAML document carrying a sops comment as
    a list element is now refused, naming the element, where it used to be
    read as an ordinary value. sops attaches a comment to the node that
    FOLLOWS it: above a mapping key it stays a `#ENC[...,type:comment]` line,
    which YAML::XS discards and sops does not hash, so such a document always
    read correctly here and still does. Above a SEQUENCE entry there is no
    comment line to write, so sops emits the comment as a real element
    (`- ENC[...,type:comment]`) and every parser keeps it. Measured against
    sops 3.13.3 on three lines of plaintext (`list:` / `# only a sequence
    comment` / `- one`): `sops -d` exit 0, File::SOPS->decrypt "MAC
    verification failed", and with ignore_mac => 1 a list holding the comment
    text as a silent extra string - which a decrypt+encrypt cycle then made a
    PERMANENT value, with `sops -d` reporting success on the result. The same
    defect turned `flow: [1, 2]  # after` into a list of two integers led by a
    string. Comments are in NO format part of sops's MAC (four measurements,
    correcting the premise recorded in karr #76), so there is no digest-only
    fix that also removes the phantom element, and this tree model has no
    place for a leaf without a key. Every read path refuses - decrypt,
    decrypt_file, extract, rotate, edit - including ignore_mac => 1, which is
    the path that produced the corruption; encrypt is untouched, since
    plaintext has no ENC values in it. The cost is stated rather than hidden:
    this is a document sops reads and File::SOPS now does not. Silently
    dropping the comment instead was measured to work and rejected, because
    every write path would then strip comments with no diagnostic at all;
    preserving them is karr #76 and would make the refusal unnecessary. See
    docs/adr/0024 and karr #108. New: File::SOPS::Encrypted->encrypted_type,
    which reads an ENC value's type label without decoding its base64.
  - BUG FIX: a YAML document whose aliases are ACYCLIC but exponentially
    shared is now refused instead of hanging the process. `l1: &l1` holding
    `a: *l0` and `b: *l0`, repeated 25 times, is 727 bytes that expand to a
    tree with 2**25 leaves; measured before the fix, encrypt took 0.06s at 9
    levels, 4.3s at 15 and never returned at 25, climbing about a gigabyte of
    RSS every three seconds. This is the half of the recursive-anchor defect
    that the ADR 0025 guard deliberately does not catch, because such a
    document really is acyclic. All eight entry points were affected in both
    directions, and so is a caller who hands `encrypt` one hash reference held
    in several places, with no YAML involved - `format => 'json'` included.
    The blowup is NOT in the parser: YAML::XS resolves an alias to the same
    reference rather than to a copy, so Load returns a linear DAG (200 levels
    in 0.7ms) and only the tree walks expand it, which is why the guard sits
    at the API boundary next to the cycle guard rather than at parse time.
    The threshold is not this distribution's: it is go-yaml's, reproduced on
    go-yaml's own counters, because an independently chosen one would refuse
    documents sops accepts. What sops budgets is a RATIO - how far the
    expansion exceeds the document as written, about 100x, tightening to about
    1.1x past four million values - and not a size: measured, it accepts a
    206,104-value expansion and refuses an 8,146-value one. Bisected against
    sops 3.13.3 in four differently shaped families, the accept/refuse
    boundary is now the same document on both sides, and the verdict matched
    the binary on 400 of 400 randomly shaped documents. A document that shares
    nothing is unaffected however large it is, and an ordinary reused anchor
    still expands to independent values as before. Cost: one extra pass over
    the distinct nodes, 3.3% of an encrypt call on a 45,000-node document. See
    docs/adr/0027 and karr #112.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and BUG FIX: a YAML document that sops wrote with a bare
    `.inf`, `-.inf` or `.nan` in an unencrypted slot can now be written BACK.
    The same release taught the parse to read such a document (see the entry
    below, docs/adr/0026): it hands back the float go-yaml resolves, carrying
    the document's own token. Every write path then refused that float, so
    `rotate` croaked on a file `sops -d` reads at exit 0, and the library ended
    up with a document class it could read and not write. The non-finite guard
    is narrowed rather than loosened: a float is written only
    where its own string half is one of the twelve plain tokens go-yaml
    resolves to exactly that double, and both halves have to agree -
    `dualvar(+Inf, '-.inf')` is a contradiction and stays refused, the same
    answer an integer whose halves disagree gets. Everything the guard was
    written for is untouched: a caller's own `9**9**9`, a computed overflow, a
    JSON literal that overflows a double, and any float whose string half is a
    spelling sops reads as a string (`.INf`, `+.nan`, `banana`). Measured
    against sops 3.13.3, all twelve tokens in an unencrypted YAML slot: `sops
    -e` -> `File::SOPS->rotate` -> `sops -d` is exit 0 with the wire
    byte-identical, where all three spellings sops writes used to croak in the
    middle. JSON is deliberately NOT carried along - it has no spelling for a
    non-finite float in either slot (measured, `sops -d` exit 51 unencrypted
    and exit 4 encrypted), and since the guard is format-blind the refusal for
    a MAC-covered document now comes from the emit walk, naming the leaf's key
    path. An ENCRYPTED slot still refuses every non-finite float in both
    formats, now with a message that says a YAML document can carry it
    unencrypted (karr #122). `edit` still cannot save such a document: the
    plaintext round trip through the editor loses the leaf's float-ness, which
    is a different defect and a different lane (karr #123). See docs/adr/0031
    and karr #113.
  - BUG FIX: a YAML document whose source used a merge key can now be read.
    sops does not expand `<<`: it unmarshals into a yaml.Node tree, where
    go-yaml resolves no merges, so the key survives and go-yaml's emitter
    writes its tag back out explicitly as `!!merge <<:`. YAML::XS accepts
    only !!str, !!int and !!float on a scalar, so such a file could not be
    OPENED here at all - `bad tag found for scalar:
    'tag:yaml.org,2002:merge'`, a parse error rather than a MAC error, on a
    document `sops -d` reads at exit 0. It was not only sops's own files:
    measured, one `sops rotate -i` on a document File::SOPS had written with
    a `<<` key added the tag, and File::SOPS could then no longer read its
    own output. The tag is now dropped from the text and the parse retried,
    but only after YAML::XS has already refused the document, and only when
    YAML::PP's parser confirms the substitution removed exactly as many
    merge-tagged scalars as the document really has - otherwise nothing is
    retried and libyaml's own error stands, so a mis-hit cannot reach the
    parser. Nothing else moves, and that was measured rather than assumed:
    the stored MAC was decrypted out of five sops-written documents, one per
    position sops writes the tag in, and reproduced leaf by leaf. `<<` is an
    ordinary key on both sides of the wire - a path component in the AAD
    (`derived:<<:x:` authenticates and `derived:x:` does not) and a member of
    the digest with its whole subtree, in document order. No AAD path, no
    digest byte and no emitted byte changes, and every document that parses
    today takes the identical path it always did. This module still writes
    the untagged spelling, which is not a loss: go-yaml assigns the merge tag
    to a plain `<<` for itself, and `sops -d` on a document we wrote as `<<:`
    prints `!!merge <<:` back. YAML::XS still does not fold `<<: *b` into its
    parent, which is what makes the round trip correct, since sops does not
    fold it either. See docs/adr/0028 and karr #116.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and BUG FIX: a YAML literal whose magnitude overflows a
    double is now the STRING sops reads there, where it used to be a float
    that had already become +Inf. `1e400`, a 401-digit integer and the bare
    spellings Inf / inf / INF / Infinity / NaN / nan / NAN / -Inf / +Inf are
    all resolved to a number by libyaml and to nothing at all by go-yaml,
    which keeps a string when strconv.ParseFloat answers ErrRange. The two
    halves of that were both broken, and the READ half is the one karr #102
    was filed without: measured against sops 3.13.3 over 20 documents sops
    writes and `sops -d` reads, `File::SOPS->decrypt` read 3 of 20 (the other
    17 failed MAC verification) and `File::SOPS->encrypt` wrote 0 of 20 (20
    non-finite croaks). Both are now 20 of 20, with sops's own `type:str` for
    the encrypted slot. The three that read before did so by coincidence -
    FormatFloat renders those three doubles as the very text their source
    token used.
    The repair is a walk in File::SOPS::Format::YAML::parse, and what makes it
    safe is a disjointness measured against the binary rather than argued: the
    only twelve tokens go-yaml resolves to a real non-finite float (.inf .Inf
    .INF +.inf +.Inf +.INF -.inf -.Inf -.INF .nan .NaN .NAN) come back from
    YAML::XS POK-ONLY, so they cannot fire a predicate that requires SVf_NOK.
    All twelve are type:float to sops and move nothing here; all 29 spellings
    that do move are type:str to sops. 27 of 105 corpus rows change, and every
    one of them is a row whose digest input was +Inf, -Inf or NaN.
    THE NON-FINITE GUARD IS UNTOUCHED: a caller who hands `encrypt` a real
    9**9**9 still gets the karr #59 refusal, in both slots. What is removed is
    an artefact of our parser, not a rule about values. JSON is deliberately
    left alone too - `sops -e` refuses an overflowing JSON number at unmarshal
    time (exit 2), so the croak there is the reference behaviour.
    Three consequences a caller can see. The unencrypted slot comes out quoted
    ('1e400') where sops writes it bare; both are a string to both parsers, so
    the digest is the same text and `sops -d` exits 0 on either. A tagged
    !!float 1e400 is retyped with the rest, because YAML::XS strips the tag -
    a divergence only in the permissive direction, on input sops itself
    refuses. And `x_unencrypted: NaN` or `-Inf` now returns the string where
    it used to return a real Perl non-finite float; that is the correction,
    since sops reads a string in that slot. An ENCRYPTED type:float whose
    plaintext is +Inf / NaN is unaffected and still decrypts to a real Perl
    infinity, byte-identical. See docs/adr/0023 and karr #102; karr #105 (a
    bare .inf sops wrote, which fails our MAC) and karr #106 (a literal that
    underflows to zero) are the neighbours it deliberately does not touch.
  - New: File::SOPS::Metadata::Flat, the flat metadata encoding the ENV and
    INI formats carry the sops section in -- sops_age__list_0__map_enc under a
    sops_ prefix in ENV, the same keys bare inside an [sops] section in INI.
    That is a second metadata wire format beside the nested mapping to_hash
    produces, not a formatting detail, and it is ONE module rather than two so
    that the two handlers cannot drift apart: the whole difference between
    them is the prefix attribute. Nothing calls it yet -- the ENV (karr #36)
    and INI (karr #37) handlers still do not exist, and this is the shared
    half both were blocked on (karr #75).
    Measured against sops 3.13.3 rather than assumed, with two recipients so
    that __list_1__ is an observation and not a guess: a map key appends
    __map_K and a list index appends __list_N, composing to any depth
    (key_groups__list_0__map_age__list_0__map_enc is five levels); indices
    must run from 0 without gaps, since sops refuses a gap with "Error while
    unflattening: Incomplete list"; the output order is a structural walk, so
    __list_10__ follows __list_9__ and is NOT a byte sort of the finished
    keys; and an empty list has no representation at all -- writing one anyway
    makes sops refuse the file ('kms[0]' expected a map or struct, got
    "string"), so they are dropped, and from_hash puts them back.
    A newline is written as backslash-n and NOTHING else is escaped -- not a
    tab, not a CR, and not a backslash -- which makes the escape lossy in one
    direction. We reproduce that rather than correcting it, because a lossless
    escape writes bytes sops misreads. See docs/adr/0022, which also records
    why the escape belongs to this layer and not to the format handlers: ENV
    applies the same transform to data values and INI does not.
    NOT decided here, and left documented as a hazard rather than closed: the
    flat formats are untyped, so unflatten hands back the STRING "false" for
    mac_only_encrypted, which is TRUE in Perl and would select the wrong
    digest. Typing them is karr #77 and it spans the wire and API lanes.
  - Diagnostics only, no document moves: the refusal for an integer above
    int64 now offers BOTH answers a caller has, where it offered one. It
    closed with "Pass it as a string to store it exactly", which was the whole
    truth until ADR 0021: past int64 a JSON number is a float64 to Go, sops
    writes type:float and the double's canonical decimal for those digits, and
    a caller who wants the leaf sops would have written wants that and not a
    string. The message now names both - the string for the digits exactly,
    and unpack('d', pack('d', $value)) for what sops writes - and says the
    second one is conditional, because it is. Measured against sops 3.13.3,
    12 literals across the window in both formats and both slots: the string
    answer is `sops -d` exit 0 with the digits verbatim in 48 of 48 cells,
    the float answer exit 0 in 41 and refused here in the other 7 - all of
    them an UNENCRYPTED YAML slot, where the decimal the emitter has to write
    is one yaml.v3 resolves as a uint64 and the YAML guard refuses it
    (docs/adr/0013). Neither answer writes a file sops rejects in any cell.
    unpack/pack is named rather than "make it a float" because the obvious
    spellings do not work: $value + 0.0 and $value * 1.0 on a Perl UV come
    back integers and land on the same croak. Recorded with it, because it is
    the half karr #101's lanes left open: this refusal can only ever be about
    a POSITIVE value. SVf_IOK means the SV holds an IV or a UV, an IV bottoms
    out at exactly int64min and a UV cannot be negative, so no integer SV
    exists below the range - measured across 14 negative decimals bracketing
    int64min and uint64max, 13 construction routes each, 182 rows, 36 of them
    int, not one below int64min and zero croaks. ADR 0021 stated that as a
    premise; it is now proved. Nothing about what is refused changed. See
    karr #104.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and BUG FIX: `rotate` no longer refuses a JSON document
    that `sops -e` wrote and `sops -d` reads. A bare JSON number between 2**63
    and 2**64-1 is an integer to Perl and a float64 to Go: sops normalises
    9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854776000 and writes type:float in both
    slots, while Cpanel::JSON::XS decoded those digits into a Perl integer, so
    detect_type called the leaf an int and assert_representable refused it with
    the int64 message - exit 255 on a file the reference implementation had
    just written, and the same refusal for `encrypt` of the plaintext it came
    from. The JSON parser now hands back for this window the same leaf karr #63
    introduced one magnitude above it: the double those digits name, carrying
    the digits as its string half, which is what YAML::XS has always returned
    and what Go reads there. Measured against sops 3.13.3 - rotating such a
    document writes the unencrypted slot back with the digits it came with and
    `sops -d` reads the result, and encrypting the plaintext writes what
    `sops -e` writes for the identical input, in an encrypted slot and an
    unencrypted one alike. A second refusal goes with it: a document whose
    unencrypted number is spelled differently from its double's canonical
    decimal - 9223372036854776832, whose double is the same 9223372036854775808
    - is one `sops -d` accepts, because Go digests the number and not the text,
    and this library used to report `MAC verification failed` on it; it now
    verifies, and decrypt_file writes it out with the normalisation `sops -d`
    writes. What an existing caller gets back moves in exactly one place, and
    it is the numeric half: decrypt and extract of an UNENCRYPTED slot still
    print the digits the document holds, but 0 + $value is now the double those
    digits name (for 9223372036854776000 that is 9223372036854775808, 192 less)
    where it used to be the digits exactly. The ENCRYPTED slot is unchanged in
    both methods - sops already wrote type:float there, so a float is what this
    library always read back - and decrypt_file's plaintext is byte-identical.
    YAML is untouched and still refuses those digits with the int64 message;
    sops cannot write such a YAML document either (`Cannot walk value, unknown
    type: uint64`, exit 23), so only JSON has a window Go reads as a number and
    Perl reads as an integer. A value in the window that did NOT come from a
    JSON parse still croaks, deliberately - the caller's scalar is an exact
    integer and no reader has spoken for it - though of 35 literals measured
    across the window 14 would have produced a document `sops -d` accepts, and
    narrowing that refusal is open as karr #104. See karr #101 and
    docs/adr/0021.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a bare JSON integer literal too wide for a Perl integer
    is now read as a FLOAT carrying its source spelling, where it used to be
    read as a string. Cpanel::JSON::XS hands such a literal back as a plain
    string SV, bit-identical to the same digits quoted, so
    `100000000000000000000` was typed str and `rotate` wrote it back into the
    document as a JSON STRING - the schema of a file the reference
    implementation had written changed, silently, and nothing failed because a
    string digests as its own text either way. There is no big integer in the
    SOPS data model: past int64 a JSON number is a float64 to Go, sops writes
    such a leaf as type:float, and YAML::XS has always returned the right shape
    here - the double with its digits as a string half. So this parser now
    gives what that one gives; nothing in the YAML handler and nothing in
    either emitter moved. A QUOTED "100000000000000000000" is unaffected and
    stays a str, told apart by the decoder's own type map and never by a
    pattern match on the text, as is every float, boolean, null and reference.
    A document this library has already written carries the value quoted, so
    it keeps verifying and reading back exactly as before. What an existing
    caller gets back does change, and in three places. decrypt hands out a
    NUMBER where it handed out a string: in an unencrypted slot that number
    still prints its digits, but in an ENCRYPTED one it is the bare NV every
    decrypted float is, so "$value" prints 1e+20 where it printed
    100000000000000000000 - extract is the method that prints all of them,
    in either slot, and it wraps this leaf the way it has wrapped every other
    float since 0.003. decrypt_file writes the leaf into the plaintext as a
    bare number rather than as a quoted string. And the digits are re-derived
    from the double, so a literal that is not its own double's canonical
    decimal (99999999999999999999, which no `sops -e` writes - sops rounds it
    to 100000000000000000000 itself) rounds the same way here. Finally, a
    literal that overflows a double now dies on every path that WRITES it -
    encrypt, encrypt_file, encrypt_in_place, rotate, edit - where it used to
    be written as a string; sops refuses the same document itself, at
    unmarshal time, and the read paths still hand the leaf back. See karr #63
    and docs/adr/0020.
  - Documentation only, no behaviour change: extract's POD said where its
    float dualvar's spelling differs from what `sops -d --extract` prints, and
    said it with one example and no boundary - so a caller could not tell
    whether their value was affected. Re-measured against sops 3.13.3 across
    the whole double range, both formats, and the answer is wider and narrower
    than the example suggested. The divergence is FORMAT-DEPENDENT: sops prints
    an exponent from a YAML document when the decimal exponent is >= 6 or < -4
    (so a plain `1000000.0` already prints as 1e+06, where this library says
    1000000), and from a JSON one only when it is >= 21 or < -6 (so the `1e20`
    the POD used as its example is `100000000000000000000` on BOTH sides
    there). The exponent's own spelling differs between the two as well - 1e-05
    in YAML, 1e-7 in JSON - so matching sops would need two formatters, not
    one. What holds everywhere: measured from 5e-324 through DBL_MAX, in both
    formats and both slot kinds, every spelling either side prints parses back
    to the identical double and no digits are lost; the positional form's cost
    is length (DBL_MAX is 309 digits, 5e-324 is `0.` and 323 zeros and a 5),
    and a -0.0 prints as -0 on both sides. The decision - the wire's spelling,
    which is the text the digest covers - is unchanged. See karr #79 and the
    amendment in docs/adr/0010.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, diagnostics only: encrypting a YAML document that has a
    `True` or `False` STRING in an unencrypted slot now WARNS, whether or not
    mac_only_encrypted is set. YAML::XS writes such a string as a bare `True`
    - libyaml's resolver knows `true` and `false` and not their titlecase
    spellings, so it does not quote them - and Go's yaml.v3 reads a BOOLEAN out
    of that token. Both sides digest the same bytes, because sops renders a
    boolean Title-cased, so the MAC holds and sops -d exits 0: the karr #86
    guard compares digest bytes and was right to accept it, and nothing in this
    library could see it. What diverges is the TYPE, and it does not survive a
    sops write-back - measured, sops 3.13.3: `sops -d --output-type json` hands
    the value on as `true`, and `sops rotate`, `sops set` and `sops edit` each
    rewrite the leaf to a bare `true`, after which this library reads a
    JSON::PP::Boolean where the caller put a string. Counter-measurement over
    91 leaves x 2 slots x both handlers = 364 rows: 0 documents stop being
    written, 0 bytes move, the same sops -d exit codes and the same values read
    back - 4 rows newly warn and all 4 really do diverge. 0 false warnings: the
    neighbours that look like this one do not warn, because measured they do
    not diverge (Yes/No/on/off/y/n are strings to both resolvers, `~` and
    `null` are written quoted, and an RFC3339 timestamp - a string here and a
    time.Time to Go - comes back from a sops write-back as the identical
    token). Not refused, unlike karr #86's class: those documents work today,
    and a caller holding the string `True` has no spelling of it this emitter
    writes quoted - encrypting the leaf or using JSON is advice, and advice
    belongs in a warning. The message names the leaf's key path and never its
    value. One check, three verdicts, not a second copy of the check. See karr
    #92 and docs/adr/0019.
  - Documentation only, no behaviour change: four POD statements described
    what this library did before 0.003. `Saying what a value is` in
    File::SOPS gave `0.0 + $x` as the way to hand encrypt() a type:float -
    which is a type:int for a whole number, because the addition sets the
    public SVf_IOK on an integral result and that flag is what the type is
    read from (measured: `0.0 + 2` and `0.0 + '2'` both detect as int). It
    now gives unpack('d', pack('d', $x)), the conversion this library makes
    on the read side for the same reason. decrypt_bytes still named `+ 0.0`
    as decrypt_value's float conversion, which karr #73 replaced.
    value_to_bytes still called the UTF-8 encoding of a character string
    "flag-guarded", the rule ADR 0003 replaced with an unconditional one -
    two paragraphs after the sentence in its own POD that says so, and
    pointing at encrypt_value, which documents the opposite. And rotate -
    the example the karr #73 entry below is written around - now says that
    an encrypted whole number keeps its type:float label across a rotation,
    which until then only decrypt_value's POD said. See karr #95 and karr
    #94; the other two carry no ticket, they were found while checking
    those.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, diagnostics only: encrypting a YAML document with
    mac_only_encrypted set now WARNS about an unencrypted leaf whose spelling
    sops resolves differently, where it used to say nothing. With that option
    the MAC covers encrypted values only, so such a leaf cannot make the
    document fail its own verification - which is why karr #86's guard was not
    installed there, and that stays: nothing is refused and no document
    changes. What was left was a divergence about a VALUE with no symptom at
    all: a `mode_unencrypted: 0755` is the integer 493 to sops and 755 here,
    sops -d exits 0, and neither implementation complains. Measured over 217
    such documents: 66 warn and every one of them really does diverge
    (0o10 -> 8, 2015-01-01 -> 2015-01-01T00:00:00Z, 1_000 -> 1000, Null ->
    null, and the .inf family, where sops writes the same spelling back but its
    own JSON dump proves it holds a float: exit 4, "json: unsupported value:
    +Inf"). 0 warn about a leaf the two agree on, and 0 documents stop being
    written. Counter-measurement over 225 leaves x 2 slots x both handlers =
    900 rows: 900 documents byte-identical before and after, 0 moved, the same
    28 croaks, and all 68 warned rows are YAML in an unencrypted slot - not one
    JSON row and not one encrypted slot. The warning names the leaf's key path
    and never its value; carp, so it reports the caller's line, and a local
    $SIG{__WARN__} silences it. It is one check with two verdicts rather than a
    second copy of the check. Documented where mac_only_encrypted is described,
    in File::SOPS::Metadata and in File::SOPS/encrypt, rather than in an ADR
    nobody looks for. See karr #87 and docs/adr/0018.
  - Internal, and no document moves: the YAML foreign-resolution guard - the
    karr #86 rule that refuses a spelling Go's parser resolves differently -
    now takes its verdict from the TOKEN this emitter writes. It used to take
    it from the leaf's stringification and to ask the emitter only once that
    stringification had already disagreed. The two are the same string for
    every leaf class but one: a boolean SV stringifies to `1` or to the empty
    string while both emitters write a bare true/false, and karr #90 reached a
    document through exactly that step - back then the digest text was `1`,
    _go_agrees("1", "1") said yes, and the guard returned before asking the
    emitter anything while the file carried `true`. The false half did not even
    reach that step, because an empty string is not a spelling Go's resolver
    looks at. Measured over 225 leaves x 2 slots x both handlers = 900 rows,
    before and after in one process: 900 identical outcomes and 0 moved, 804
    written and 96 refused on both sides, sops -d exit 0 on all 804. The price
    is one emitter probe per leaf Go's resolver looks at - +2.4ms on a 400-leaf
    document - while a string it ignores still stops at the gate for 0.8ms per
    1000, and a boolean got cheaper (24.9ms -> 17.5ms per 1000). No message
    text and no argument changes. See karr #91 and docs/adr/0017.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, and a fix: PERL'S OWN BOOLEAN was written as an integer.
    Perl has no boolean type, but since 5.36 it has a boolean SV - `!!1`,
    `!!0`, `$x > 3`, `'a' eq 'a'`, `defined $x`, `builtin::true` and every
    comparison's result carry it, and the mark survives assignment and storage
    in a hash. It publishes the public SVf_IOK, so detect_type called it an int
    and the MAC digest covered `1`/`0` - while BOTH emitters write such an SV
    as a bare true/false. The document said one thing and its own MAC covered
    another, and the route in is ordinary caller code:
    `encrypt(data => { admin => ($user->{level} > 3) })`, or a tree the caller
    loaded with their own YAML::XS::Load (our parse localises
    $YAML::XS::Boolean, a caller's does not). Measured against sops 3.13.3 over
    ten such leaves x two slots x both handlers, before: 12 documents sops -d
    accepted while handing back the integer 1, 20 it refused with exit 51, and
    8 this library refused to write. After: 40 of 40 exit 0, reading back true
    and false. Such a scalar is now type:bool with a plaintext of True/False,
    which is byte-for-byte what `sops -e` writes for a plaintext `admin: true`.
    The predicate is builtin::is_bool - the same one both emitters use, and the
    only way to reach SvIsBOOL from Perl. Not a flag lookalike: measured,
    dualvar(1, '1') carries the same IV, the same PV and the same PUBLIC flags
    as !!1, and both emitters write it as `1`, so a B-based test would have
    typed it bool and introduced this defect while fixing it. Nothing looks at
    the value's text - this is a mark Perl put on the scalar, read the way
    SVf_IOK is read. On a perl older than 5.36 there is no such SV for either
    emitter to recognise, and detect_type still answers int there. A second
    mechanism, not in the ticket, is closed with it: a FALSE sentinel in an
    ENCRYPTED slot reached the document as a plaintext '' against a digest of
    False, because _encrypt_tree skips a leaf that is an empty string and
    `!!0`'s PV is the empty string - the same defect the JSON->false guard
    beside it was added for, one value class over, and the !blessed() test does
    not cover a sentinel. Measured over a 192-row emitter corpus (48 leaves x 2
    slots x both handlers), before and after: 40 rows move and every one holds
    a boolean sentinel; 0 of the other 152 change, in either format or either
    slot. Wire bytes DO move for one case that verified before: an encrypted
    `!!1` was type:int with a plaintext of 1 and sops -d handed the caller back
    the integer 1 for a value they wrote as a boolean; it is now type:bool, and
    a round trip through this library returns JSON->true rather than 1. The
    read path is untouched - a type:bool still comes back a JSON::PP::Boolean,
    and an older type:int document still reads back as the integer it says it
    is. The two boolean routes now write IDENTICAL documents in both formats
    and both slots, sentinel and JSON->true alike. Five encrypts of the same
    tree in one process produce one document and leave every neighbouring
    scalar's type untouched. The karr #86 guard becomes consistent as a side
    effect: it used to compare Go's resolution of the leaf's STRINGIFICATION
    (`1`) against our digest text (`1`), agree, and return before asking the
    emitter which token it writes - the one leaf class where those two are
    different strings. With the digest text now True it asks, gets `true`,
    resolves it to True and agrees for the right reason. karr #90, docs/adr/0016.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, and a fix: a NEGATIVE ZERO on which Perl had cached an
    integer was written as the integer 0. Perl sets the public SVf_IOK on a
    float whenever the cast back round-trips, and for -0.0 it decides the cast
    round-trips while the SIGN DOES NOT SURVIVE IT, so detect_type said int
    and the MAC digest covered 0 for a value whose wire form is -0. Two doors
    in, and the second is why this is not a YAML spelling rule: YAML::XS
    caches that integer for an integral float in EXPONENT notation, so -0.0e0,
    -0e0, -0.0E+0, -0.000e2, -0.0e-5, -0e-0, -0.0e+0, -00.0e0, -0.e0 and -.0e0
    all arrived carrying it where a plain -0.0 did not; and any $v == 0,
    $v > 1, int($v) or sprintf('%d', $v) on a caller's OWN -0.0 sets it in
    place, before encrypt() ever sees the tree. So whether the document said
    -0 or 0 depended on whether the caller had looked at the value. Such a
    scalar is now a float, decided on the NV's bit pattern where that half is
    already public - no text is inspected, and nothing is numified to ask.
    The karr #86 guard could not see any of it, and that is the part worth
    knowing: Format::YAML::_go_float modelled Go with value_to_bytes($p * 1.0)
    and Perl's arithmetic settles "-0.0e0" on its INTEGER path, so the model
    answered 0, this module answered 0, and the guard confirmed the defect
    instead of catching it. That conversion is now unpack('d', pack('d', $p)),
    the copy karr #88 measured; fixing only the model would have turned 12
    silently-broken documents into 12 croaks without making one of them
    writable. REPAIRED rather than refused, because sops itself cannot write
    this value unencrypted either - `sops -e` on a plaintext -0.0 emits -0 and
    then rejects its own file with exit 51 - so there is nothing to tell a
    caller to pass instead, while -0.0 is a spelling both implementations read
    as the double the digest covers (karr #62). Wire bytes DO move, for this
    value only: an encrypted negative zero is now type:float with a plaintext
    of -0, which is byte-for-byte what sops -e writes for the same source, and
    sops -d reads -0 where it read 0. Measured over a 324-row emitter corpus
    (81 leaves x 2 slots x both handlers), before and after: 260 rows are
    byte-identical, 64 move and every one of them holds a negative zero - 12
    were sops -d exit 51 and are now exit 0, 12 were a croak in JSON and are
    now written as -0.0, 40 kept working with different bytes - and 0 rows
    that worked stop working. 1e3, 1.0e0, 0755e0, 0.0e0 and -1.0e0 stay
    type:int: their two halves agree about the value, and reading SVf_NOK
    first instead would have moved 50 further rows to fix the one that needed
    it. Three encrypts of the same tree in the same process produce the same
    leaf and the same sops -d output, every round. See karr #89 and
    docs/adr/0015; ADR 0013 carries an amendment for its model.
  - POD: say that every YAML file this distribution writes begins with the
    document-start marker ---, where sops writes none. YAML::XS's emitter
    always emits it, so it is line 1 of the encrypted document and of the
    plaintext alike. Cosmetic and not a compatibility difference - YAML
    resolves a document with or without it identically, sops -d accepts our
    files, and the MAC covers values rather than serialized text - but it was
    nowhere in the POD, and a caller diffing our output against sops -d met it
    with no warning. Documented rather than removed: stripping it means
    changing the emitter the MAC's encrypt side rides on. See karr #83.
  - Fix: a refusal now reports the line the CALLER wrote encrypt(), emit() or
    decrypt() on, instead of a line inside this distribution. croak names the
    caller of the frame it stands in, and every frame between a caller and the
    guards is our own - File::SOPS::encrypt calls emit(), emit() calls
    Encrypted->canonical_float_tree, and the walk calls the handler's guards
    back - so a refused leaf ended "at lib/File/SOPS/Encrypted.pm line NNN",
    which is what the house rule "croak, never die" exists to prevent. The
    same applied to "Could not decrypt data key with any of the provided
    identities", which named a line in SOPS.pm. Fixed with @CARP_NOT in
    Format::YAML, Format::JSON and Backend::Age. Nothing a message SAYS
    changes: measured over 32 refusal paths, 10 locations move and all 10 move
    out of lib/, while 0 message texts differ - the key paths karr #68 added
    are untouched, and a test now asserts both in the same message. See
    karr #71.
  - Fix: File::SOPS::Encrypted->value_to_bytes returned text in a scalar that
    also carried the number that text spells, so detect_type called its own
    output a float - and an int for the text -0, which numifies to an IV. The
    shortest-form search in _float_bytes compared with `$g + 0`, which
    numifies in place: the same in-place retyping karr #72 and #73 fixed on
    the input side, this time on the output side. It bit the caller who sends
    a value through value_to_bytes and hands the result back to encrypt, where
    a string is what they meant and a float is what they got - a different
    type on the wire and a different digest. The comparison now stringifies
    first, so the digits leave as digits. No wire bytes move: measured over
    18216 value_to_bytes rows (3036 leaves x 6 type labels, including 3000
    random doubles) and a 228-row emitter corpus, 0 differences. See karr #80.
  - Fix: a negative zero that came out of a YAML parse and is written as JSON
    died in the float carrier with a message that named the wrong cause -
    "Math::BigFloat did not render a float leaf at full precision. Check for a
    global Math::BigFloat->accuracy or ->precision setting", with no such
    setting in play. Math::BigFloat has no signed zero, so new('-0')
    stringifies as 0 and the assertion fired on a sign it cannot carry. Read a
    YAML file, write it as JSON is ordinary caller code, and it was the one
    cell of four that failed: the same value written from a bare Perl NV, and
    the same value written as YAML, have always worked. A negative zero is now
    carried by the double itself, which Cpanel::JSON::XS writes as -0.0 -
    byte-identical to what a bare NV of that value already produced, and the
    only spelling that works: measured against sops 3.13.3, a JSON leaf of
    -0.0 is exit 0 and read back as -0, while the canonical -0 is exit 51,
    because Go reads a JSON -0 as an INTEGER and digests 0. The same split
    karr #62 measured in YAML. Nothing that worked changes: of a 228-row
    emitter corpus, 6 rows move and every one of them was a croak; no document
    changes bytes in either format. Of 2018 canonical texts from
    value_to_bytes, -0 is the only one Math::BigFloat does not reproduce, so
    the branch is that narrow. See karr #88 and docs/adr/0014.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a YAML leaf whose SPELLING Go's parser resolves
    differently from libyaml is now refused when a document that carries a MAC
    is written. sops parses with gopkg.in/yaml.v3, whose resolver strips `_`
    from a number, reads 0x / 0b / 0o and a LEADING ZERO as octal, and tries
    four timestamp layouts - none of which libyaml (YAML::XS) does. So the
    digest covered the value this module resolved while the document carried a
    spelling sops re-read as another one. Measured against sops 3.13.3, leaf
    under _unencrypted: `mode: 0755` is 755 here and 493 there, 010 is 10 and
    8, 0o10 is the string "0o10" and the integer 8, and 0x1f, 0b101, 1_000,
    .inf, .nan, Null, TRUE and a bare 2015-01-01 all exit 51 the same way. The
    file was written silently and rejected later with MAC mismatch, naming
    nothing. What this closes is measured, not exhaustive: every spelling
    named here was put through sops 3.13.3 before and after, and the guard's
    model of Go's resolver is verified against the binary over that corpus -
    a spelling nobody has fed to both parsers is still a spelling nobody has
    measured. Nothing that worked stops working: of a 648-row emitter corpus,
    53 rows move and every one of them already failed
    - 50 with sops -d exit 51 and 3 with exit 25, a uint64 sops refuses to
    write at all - while 007, 08, 1e3, True, null, yes, 1:30, _7, 0o8,
    123abc, 2015-01-01T12:00:00Z and 2015-01-01T12:00:00.5Z are byte-identical
    before and after, exit 0. 007 is not refused because 7 is 7 in both bases;
    a guard on leading zeros alone would be wrong in both directions, since it
    would also miss 0o10, which this module types as a string. The message
    names the leaf's key path, never the value, and says what to pass instead:
    sops itself resolves a plaintext `mode: 0755` to the integer 493 and
    writes that. Untouched: encrypted slots (an ENC[...] string carries any
    spelling verbatim), JSON (which quotes every string and has no octal), the
    plaintext emitters used by decrypt_file and edit (no MAC to disagree
    with), the sops metadata section (not covered by the digest) and
    mac_only_encrypted documents (where the digest does not cover an
    unencrypted leaf). See karr #86 and docs/adr/0013.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: an integer leaf that carries its own, DIFFERENT string
    form is now refused at emit time, in both formats. detect_type reads the
    public SVf_IOK first, so such a scalar - a Scalar::Util::dualvar, or a
    value a YAML parser kept the source spelling of - is an int and the MAC
    digest covers the NUMBER, while both emitters write the STRING half:
    YAML::XS bare, Cpanel::JSON::XS quoted whenever that half differs from
    its own rendering of the number. The document and its own MAC then state
    different things and the file is unreadable to sops and to this module
    alike, written silently. Measured against sops 3.13.3, leaf under
    _unencrypted: dualvar(5,'five') and dualvar(0,'zero') exit 51 in YAML and
    JSON, and a 007, +7, -0 or 1e3 read out of a YAML document exits 51 once
    written as JSON. Nothing caught it - the float walk only inspected float
    leaves. The emitter is now asked about such a leaf, through the same
    measured round-trip the float branch uses, and the leaf is refused where
    the emitter does not write the text the digest covers; the message names
    the leaf's key path and neither half of the value. Nothing that worked
    stops working: all 14 newly refused documents failed their own MAC
    before, and YAML still writes a source spelling (007, +7, -0, 1e3) back
    faithfully at exit 0, byte for byte. Encrypted slots, decrypted trees and
    the plaintext emitters are untouched. Refused rather than repaired
    because both halves are a candidate for what the caller meant and nothing
    measurable separates a spelling from a contradiction - dualvar(0,'zero')
    numifies to the very number it would be compared against. See karr #84
    and docs/adr/0012.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, and a fix: a float leaf that carries its own string
    form reached a JSON document as a QUOTED STRING. Cpanel::JSON::XS writes
    a scalar with a public string half as a JSON string whenever that half
    differs from its own rendering of the number, and Format::JSON's
    round-trip check could not see it: it reparsed the quoted string,
    value_to_bytes re-derived that same text from it, and the two compared
    equal. Nothing failed - the digest covers the canonical decimal either
    way, so the MAC held and `sops -d` exited 0 handing back a string where
    a number was passed. Two ordinary routes in: extract() returns such a
    dualvar for a float leaf, and YAML::XS keeps the source text of every
    scalar it parses, so any float from a YAML document carries one on its
    way into JSON. The round-trip check now answers no for such a leaf and
    the existing Math::BigFloat carrier writes the canonical decimal as a
    BARE NUMBER - the document YAML has always produced for the same value,
    and, for the 17-digit case, byte-identical to the one a plain number
    produces. Measured against sops 3.13.3: 28 of 28 documents exit 0 with
    the same double read back, and a 244-row corpus through both handlers
    moves 20 rows, all of them this case, none in YAML and none in the ADR
    0005 / ADR 0006 float set. See karr #78 and docs/adr/0011.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: extract() now returns a float that prints all of its
    digits. It handed
    back the Perl scalar it found, and a decrypted float is a bare NV with no
    string form of its own, so every stringification went through Perl's 15
    significant digits. Measured on a document the real sops wrote, both
    formats: `sops -d --extract` printed 0.30000000000000004 where our
    extract printed 0.3. Nothing failed - the document, the plaintext and the
    MAC were all correct; only this return value was, and only once the
    caller printed it. A float leaf now comes back as a dualvar: numerically
    the same double, as a string the canonical decimal the document holds.
    Arithmetic, ==, sprintf '%f' and is_deeply are unchanged; `"$value"` is
    not, and that is the fix. Two boundaries are deliberate: the spelling is
    the wire's, so 1e20 stringifies as 100000000000000000000 where sops
    prints 1e+20 (same double, no digits lost, karr #79), and only the leaf
    extract returns is wrapped - floats inside a returned branch, and
    everything decrypt() returns, stay plain scalars, because a dualvar in a
    tree changes what the emitters write (karr #78). See karr #61 (second
    half) and docs/adr/0010.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the type:float label is now kept on an encrypted
    whole number. The READ path
    converted a type:float plaintext with `$data + 0.0`, which sets the
    public SVf_IOK flag whenever the result is integral, so detect_type
    called the value an int and the next write relabelled the leaf
    type:int - on a document sops itself had written. Measured, both
    formats: `sops -e` of `whole: 2.0` came back out of our rotate as
    type:int, three of five type:float leaves relabelled, `sops -d` exit 0
    before and after and printing `whole: 2` both times. Nothing failed,
    which is why it lived: the plaintext is `2` under either label and the
    digest covers `2` either way. What changed was the document's own type
    field, silently. The conversion is now
    `unpack('d', pack('d', $plaintext))`, which goes through the float64 Go
    parses into and leaves the scalar a float. Two consequences are
    deliberate and measured: a plaintext carrying more integer precision
    than a double can hold (9007199254740993) now re-derives the double Go
    also reads (9007199254740992) instead of an exact integer under a wrong
    label, and decrypt_file writes an integral float as `2.0` in JSON, where
    it wrote `2` before and `sops -d` writes `2` - `2.0` parses back as a
    float, so decrypt_file, edit, encrypt_file keeps the leaf a type:float.
    The YAML side still writes `2`, exactly as sops does. See karr #73 and
    docs/adr/0009.
  - Pin decrypt_file's handling of an ENCRYPTED float against the sops
    binary. No code change: the ADR 0006 emitter fix (karr #58) already made
    both formats write the canonical decimal, so a document sops wrote with
    ENC[...,type:float] 0.30000000000000004 now comes back out of
    decrypt_file with all 17 digits where it used to lose them silently
    (measured: 0.3, both formats, nothing failing). The value simply came out
    wrong, so anyone doing decrypt_file, hand-edit, encrypt_file had changed
    the number without being told. The relapse this guards against is a
    future emitter rebuild, which is why the assertion is on the literal
    digits on the wire rather than on a round-trip through our own reader -
    an emitter that writes 0.3 and a reader that parses 0.3 agree with each
    other. t/24-float-precision.t section 11. See karr #61 (first half; the
    second half is the extract() entry at the top of this release).
  - Fix an encrypted negative zero losing its sign on the READ path. A
    type:float plaintext was converted with `$data + 0.0`, which is positive
    zero twice over: Perl settles the text -0 as an INTEGER zero, which has no
    sign to keep, and IEEE round-to-nearest makes even a genuine -0.0 + 0.0
    come out +0.0. Go's strconv.ParseFloat("-0", 64) is negative zero. The MAC
    could not catch it - the digest covers decrypt_bytes, the plaintext "-0",
    not the deserialized value - so every document verified while our own
    rotate turned `negzero: -0` into `negzero: 0`, exit 0, in both formats and
    without a word. Unlike the unencrypted case of karr #62, sops CAN write
    this value: an encrypted leaf never reaches its float emitter,
    so `sops -e` on a plaintext -0.0 produces a document it reads back at exit
    0. Measured against sops 3.13.3, both formats, starting from that fixture:
    decrypt now keeps the sign, rotate leaves `-0` where sops wrote it, and
    decrypt_file writes it out too. Nothing else on the float read ladder
    moves: 33 type:float plaintexts through the conversion and back out
    through value_to_bytes, the five spellings of negative zero change and the
    other 28 rows are identical. Pinned by t/24-float-precision.t section 10,
    against the binary. See karr #72 and the karr #72 amendment in
    docs/adr/0006.
  - Fix a YAML document carrying an unencrypted negative zero. YAML::XS
    renders an NV -0.0 as `0` while the MAC digest covers `-0`, so the
    document and its own MAC stated different numbers: self-MAC FAIL,
    `sops -d` exit 51, written without a word. It was thought to have no
    YAML representation, because emitting the canonical `-0` makes Go's
    yaml.v3 resolve an INTEGER zero and digest `0` - still a mismatch.
    Measured against sops 3.13.3, `-0.0` is a spelling that works: exit 0,
    read back as -0, self-MAC OK. The YAML emitter now writes that. JSON is
    untouched - Cpanel::JSON::XS already wrote -0.0 and that row has always
    worked - and so is every other value: 31 floats, strings and integers
    emitted through both handlers before and after move exactly one line.
    An encrypted -0.0 was never affected (it is an ENC[...] string by the
    time the emitter runs) and still is not. Note that sops cannot write
    this value either: `sops -e` on a plaintext -0.0 emits `-0` and then
    rejects its own file with exit 51, in YAML and in JSON alike. Pinned by
    t/24-float-precision.t section 9, against the binary. See karr #62 and
    the karr #62 amendment in docs/adr/0006.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a non-finite float (NaN, +Inf, -Inf) is now refused
    at encrypt time instead of written into a document nothing can read
    back. File::SOPS::Encrypted::value_to_bytes has always written
    +Inf / -Inf / NaN - the same text Go's strconv.FormatFloat produces -
    and the MAC digest covered that text, but no emitter could carry it.
    Measured against sops 3.13.3: Cpanel::JSON::XS writes null (the value
    is simply gone, self-MAC FAIL, sops -d exit 51), JSON::XS writes bare
    inf (invalid JSON, sops refuses with "invalid character i"), and
    YAML::XS writes bare Inf or NaN (exit 51 for Inf; NaN happened to
    work, because Go reads bare NaN as the STRING NaN whose ToBytes is
    also NaN). All three put a file on disk that at least one
    implementation cannot read back, and the caller had no reason to
    notice. The guard lives in assert_representable, next to the int64
    range check and the reference checks; the error names the form
    (+Inf, -Inf, NaN), never the value, and says to store the number as
    a string instead - that is type:str, written verbatim, and it
    round-trips exactly through both implementations. -0.0 is NOT
    refused: it is finite, and the JSON -0.0 row is the one ADR 0005
    went out of its way to protect. Reading is unchanged - a type:float
    plaintext of +Inf or NaN is still accepted by _deserialize_value,
    because sops itself writes it, so the read path has to keep taking
    it. Pinned by t/28-non-finite-float-refusal.t. See karr #59.
  - Refuse a data key from Crypt::Age that is not 32 bytes long, in
    File::SOPS::Backend::Age::decrypt_data_key. The data key the SOPS
    data path consumes is exactly the 32-byte AES-256 key every value
    in the document is encrypted under, and a 16- or 24-byte return
    would be silently accepted by CryptX as a working AES-128 or
    AES-192 key -- the encrypt-side CSPRNG checks (File::SOPS::Encrypted
    ::_random_bytes) close the same defect class on the *generator* side
    and this is its symmetric pair on the *consumer* side. The check
    names the length received and the length expected; it never names
    the key material. Crypt::Age 0.001 returns 32 bytes, and we cannot
    rely on that -- the data key crosses an opaque CSPRNG boundary we
    cannot see. Pinned by t/21-random-bytes.t (three new subtests: the
    refusal covers 16/24/31/33/40, the error carries lengths not key
    material, and a real round-trip with a 32-byte data key still
    works). See karr #52.
  - POD: tell callers that JSON->true / JSON->false need
    `use JSON::MaybeXS qw(JSON);` in the calling code. The example used
    to work under a bare `use File::SOPS;` by accident (CryptX loads
    JSON.pm on the way to Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM, so the JSON class-method
    call resolves), but the example should not rely on that -- it is not
    under this distribution's control, and a future CryptX that stops
    loading JSON.pm silently breaks the bool example. The "Value types"
    section now says so explicitly, and the "Saying what a value is"
    section shows the use-line above the examples. No behaviour change.
    See karr #57.
  - Document what creation_rules_for deliberately does not read or
    return (karr #54): destination_rules is not consulted (File::SOPS
    does not move files); a rule with aws_kms / azure_kv / hc_vault is
    ignored (those are the sops section field names, which sops itself
    ignores in a creation rule, not the config-file field names the
    refusal names); $SOPS_CONFIG is not consulted unless the caller
    passes config explicitly. All three are now called out under "What
    this does not read or return" in the POD. No behaviour change.
  - Refuse a path_regex that uses a construct Go RE2 does not support, at
    match time. creation_rules_for compiled the same string as Perl here
    and as Go RE2 in sops, and a pattern that compiles here but uses
    lookarounds or backreferences silently selects a different rule (or
    none) in sops without either side saying so. Lookarounds
    ((?= / (?! / (?<= / (?<!), backreferences (\1..\9), and anything else
    that is unambiguously RE2-incompatible are now refused here, naming
    the construct and the config file. (?i), character classes, standard
    quantifiers and anchors compile in both and pass. See karr #53.
  - Note the deliberate structural coupling in File::SOPS::edit:
    _is_sops_not_a_mapping keys on File::SOPS::Metadata::from_hash's
    exception text to tell a reserved-key refusal from a parse failure.
    A structural fix (syntax-only parse in the format handlers, leaving
    the sops section in the tree) reaches well past the one call site
    that needs the distinction and into the format lane (karr #51). The
    coupling is documented at the call site and pinned by t/17-in-place-
    and-edit.t in both directions -- all four shapes of a hand-written
    sops entry get the reserved-key message, broken YAML gets the parse
    message, and a Metadata.pm rewording that breaks the classifier fails
    a test rather than silently restoring the regression. No code change.
  - Document the deliberate "match sops" decision on decrypt_file output
    permissions: a new file is created with 0666 & ~umask (the open '>' rule),
    not with a hard 0600. The "How a file is written" POD now records that
    the alternative was considered and rejected (karr #45): it would break a
    caller whose next step is another process reading the file, and would
    diverge from the reference implementation without a measurable security
    gain over a caller-set umask 077. No behaviour change.
  - Document the deliberate divergence: edit re-keys the file, sops edit does
    not. The "Editing re-keys the file" section in File::SOPS::edit's POD now
    points forward to the future data_key => $bytes argument that would close
    the gap, and notes that the cost of closing it is raw key material on the
    public API, so it is an API decision rather than a refactor. No behaviour
    change; the implementation has said this in a comment since edit shipped,
    but a one-line POD tweak makes it a contract. See karr #41.
  - Pin the parser-disagreement fidelity gap against the real sops binary.
    A handful of bare scalars -- 0x10, 1_000 in YAML, a JSON integer above
    2^64 -- resolve to different types between Go's yaml.v3 / encoding/json
    and this distribution's YAML::XS / Cpanel::JSON::XS. Both directions
    round-trip self-consistently and neither is a MAC failure: a document
    File::SOPS writes is accepted by sops and vice versa. The value comes
    back as a different type than sops would have produced for the same
    input (the YAML 0x10 comes back as the string "0x10", not the int 16;
    a JSON int above 2^64 round-trips as a string here while sops would
    have truncated it to a float). The gap is recorded rather than fixed
    in ADR 0002 ("Type detection now depends on the parser, so parsers
    can disagree") and is now pinned by an interop subtest so a future
    attempt to close it has to break that assertion on purpose. No code
    or wire bytes move.
  - A refused leaf is now named by its key path. When the YAML or JSON
    emitter refuses a leaf it cannot write as the text the digest covers
    - a blessed object, a Math::BigFloat, an unblessed \1 - the message
    opens with where that leaf sits: "servers:1:weight: cannot write ..."
    or "(document root): cannot write ...". It named the class and the
    cause but not the location, so one bad leaf in a large document was
    a manual search (karr #68). The path is built by the same walk that
    finds the leaf (File::SOPS::Encrypted::canonical_float_tree, whose
    optional reject callback is now called as $reject->($leaf, $where)),
    and it is the shape the MAC walk's own messages already use, so
    there is one notation to learn and not two. Array indices are
    carried here where the MAC path drops them - that omission is the
    AAD rule, and this string is a diagnostic. Keys only: no value ever
    reaches the message. No wire bytes move; the walk returns the same
    tree it did before, verified against sops 3.13.3.
  - Format::JSON's reject callback is renamed from _reject_foreign_bignum
    to _reject_referenced_leaf. Internal, no API change: the two-name
    Math::BigFloat / Math::BigInt whitelist it was named for stopped
    existing when karr #66 widened it to every reference but an exact
    JSON::PP::Boolean, and the name had been describing the old rule
    ever since. The YAML twin is _reject_unwritable_leaf.
  IMPORTANT - if you encrypted data with 0.001 or 0.002, read this.
  The defects below could corrupt or misrepresent stored values, and
  two of them are not repairable from the existing ciphertext:
    * A false boolean was written as an unencrypted empty value. Such a
      document also fails its own MAC check, so this release may refuse
      to decrypt it at all - and the original value is gone, replaced by
      a placeholder indistinguishable from an empty string.
    * The string 'false' was encrypted as True - a silent value
      inversion. It decrypts without error and returns the wrong value.
    * No YAML file written by 0.001 or 0.002 could be read by the sops
      CLI. Those files are intact and are readable again once
      re-encrypted with this version.
    * A document with a key in the Latin-1 range (a key containing e.g.
      é or ö) was written with a Latin-1 AAD where sops uses UTF-8.
      Neither sops nor this release can decrypt such a value; the
      ciphertext is intact but recovering it needs the old AAD. Keys
      above U+00FF could not be written at all - they died. Values
      under ASCII keys are unaffected.
    * A VALUE in the Latin-1 range that was passed as an unflagged Perl
      string was written to the wire as Latin-1 where sops uses UTF-8.
      Such documents still decrypt and still verify under this release -
      the ciphertext is intact and the MAC covers those same bytes - but
      the value comes back as bytes rather than characters, and sops
      reads it as a binary scalar rather than as the text you stored.
      Re-encrypting with this release writes it correctly. The same
      value under unencrypted_suffix produced a document that failed
      verification when it was written; there is nothing to recover
      there, and it must be rewritten from the plaintext.
    * encrypt_file on a file that was ALREADY encrypted encrypted every
      value a second time and wrote the result, silently - and with
      output defaulting to input, over the original. THE VALUES ARE
      GONE. Parsing split the existing sops section off and the new one
      replaced it, so the file that was written holds the inner
      ENC[...] strings under a NEW data key while the data key they
      were encrypted with is not in the file at all. Decrypting such a
      file returns the inner ENC[...] strings; nothing can decrypt
      those. If you have a copy of the file from before it was
      re-encrypted, its sops section holds the missing key.
    * A top-level key literally named 'sops' in the data you encrypted
      was overwritten by the metadata section. The value is gone, and
      the document fails its own MAC because the digest covered it.
    * A null value was written as an empty string, so a key whose value
      was null came back as '' and reached sops as '' too. Repairable
      by re-encrypting from the plaintext.
    * READING was affected too, whoever wrote the file: decrypt could
      hand back a type:float an order of magnitude out - a stored
      100000000000000000000 returned as 1e21 - roughly one leaf in a
      hundred, with no error, with the MAC verifying, and NOT
      reproducibly, so the same file read twice could give two answers.
      The stored documents are intact and this release reads them
      correctly; what may be wrong is anything DERIVED from a value read
      with 0.001 or 0.002 - a number copied into another system, written
      to a second file, or used in a calculation whose result was kept.
      Nothing in the encrypted file needs repairing; the destinations do.
  Re-encrypt affected documents from their ORIGINAL PLAINTEXT, not from
  the stored ciphertext. Where a value gated access or behaviour, verify
  it by hand: a wrong value may have been in effect since it was written.

  - Fix an UNENCRYPTED float that needs 16 or 17 significant digits
    being written with 15, so the document stated one number and the
    MAC covered another and the file failed its own verification - here
    and in sops (MAC mismatch, exit 51). value_to_bytes writes a float
    as Go does, the shortest decimal that round-trips; both emitters
    rendered a double with 15 (Cpanel::JSON::XS through %.15g, YAML::XS
    through Perl stringification). 0.1+0.2 went in as 0.3 and 1/3 as
    0.333333333333333. Each emitter now reparses its own output and
    substitutes the canonical decimal only where the value does not come
    back as the same double, so no value that already worked moves a
    single byte - verified case by case against sops 3.13.3, including
    the -0.0 that ADR 0005 paid for. See docs/adr/0006.
    Three consequences worth knowing. Hand-typed decimals never
    triggered this (0.1, 1.5, 3.14 all survive 15 digits); computed ones
    almost always do, so the documents affected are the machine-written
    ones. rotate and encrypt_in_place on a JSON file that SOPS ITSELF
    wrote used to destroy such a value and produce a file sops rejects -
    YAML escaped that because YAML::XS happens to keep the text it
    parsed. And edit did not fail at all: it degraded the value through
    the plaintext round-trip before the digest was taken, so the file
    verified with the wrong number in it. All three are fixed, and
    decrypt_file stops losing precision as a side effect - it now writes
    what sops -d writes. extract still hands back a Perl float and is
    unchanged. NaN, Inf and a YAML -0.0 are a separate defect and keep
    today's behaviour.
    New prerequisite: Math::BigFloat (core). It is the only carrier
    measured to reach JSON as a bare number rather than a quoted string.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a YAML document with a REFERENCE as a leaf value is
    now refused instead of written. YAML::XS writes a blessed reference
    as a Perl-specific tagged structure - !!perl/hash:Math::BigFloat,
    !!perl/scalar:Foo, !!perl/regexp - and an unblessed one as
    !!perl/ref or !!perl/code, while detect_type calls the leaf str so
    the MAC digest covers its STRINGIFICATION. The document and its own
    MAC then stated different things and nothing could read the file:
    measured against sops 3.13.3, every such document failed its own
    verification here and all but one failed in sops too (exit 51). The
    exception is qr//, which sops accepts because Go resolves the
    unknown tag to the scalar text - and which File::SOPS then cannot
    read back, because YAML::XS rebuilds a Regexp from it. It was
    written silently in every case. The JSON handler has refused these
    leaves all along (Cpanel::JSON::XS will not encode an object without
    allow_blessed), so this is the same answer in both formats rather
    than a new rule. See docs/adr/0008.
    Nothing that worked stops working. An exact JSON::PP::Boolean is
    still written as bare true/false - that is the one reference whose
    document form and digest agree, and it is what JSON->true and a
    YAML true/false already produce, including the mac_only_encrypted
    flag in the sops section. A SUBCLASS of it is refused: detect_type
    calls it bool but YAML::XS writes it as a tag. And only leaves that
    reach the document verbatim can trigger this at all - values
    excluded by the encryption rules, plaintext documents, the sops
    section. An object in an ENCRYPTED slot is an ENC[...] string by the
    time the emitter sees it and still stores its stringification as
    type:str, unchanged.
    The error names the class and what to pass instead; it never names
    the value. See karr #65.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a JSON document with a REFERENCE as a leaf value is
    now refused instead of written, closing the "known gap" the YAML guard
    above left open. Cpanel::JSON::XS under allow_bignum writes a
    Math::BigFloat or Math::BigInt as a bare JSON number, and an unblessed
    SCALAR ref as bare true or false - the documented JSON::XS convention
    for \1 / \0, not a Cpanel bug. detect_type calls every reference but a
    JSON::PP::Boolean str, so the MAC digest covers the leaf's
    STRINGIFICATION, and the document and its own MAC then stated different
    things: \1 / \0 digests as SCALAR(0x...) (a heap address) while the
    document carries true or false. Measured against sops 3.13.3, every
    such document failed its own verification here and in sops -d (exit
    51), written silently. The exception is an exact JSON::PP::Boolean,
    which Cpanel writes as bare true/false - the one reference whose
    document form and digest agree. A SUBCLASS of it is refused: detect_type
    calls it bool but neither Cpanel nor the carrier pipeline writes it as
    bare true/false, and the same defect was wearing the whitelist. Same
    exception as on the YAML side, same exact-class rule, same "encrypted
    slots are unaffected" rule, same rule that the error names the class
    and never the value. See docs/adr/0008 (closes the "known gap, karr
    #66" it explicitly filed).
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: an unblessed reference in an ENCRYPTED slot is now
    refused at encrypt time instead of silently stored as a heap address.
    The previous behaviour wrote ENC[...,type:str] whose plaintext was the
    reference's stringification - SCALAR(0x...) / CODE(0x...) - and the
    MAC digest covered that same stringification, so the file verified
    (doc and digest agreed) but stored a value that differs per process
    run and is meaningless on a later read. Nothing in the pre-fix
    document signalled that the value would not survive a second
    invocation. The new guard lives in assert_representable, sitting next
    to the int64 check and exempting every blessed reference; this is the
    same blessed-vs-unblessed split ADR 0008 measured for the encrypted
    slot's happy path (a Math::BigFloat, an object overloading "", a qr//
    - all blessed, all round-tripping correctly). An ARRAY or HASH ref
    in an encrypted slot still works: the tree walks recurse into an
    unblessed HASH or ARRAY before any leaf branch fires, so the ref
    itself never reaches assert_representable and the contents are
    encrypted normally. The error names the ref kind (unblessed SCALAR
    reference, unblessed CODE reference, ...) and never the value; it
    says to pass a string or a blessed object whose stringification is
    the value meant. Reading is unchanged: an older 0.003 file this
    library wrote with an unblessed ref in an encrypted slot is read
    back as the heap address that was stored, which is unrecognisable
    but verifiable - the guard refuses new writes and leaves the read
    path alone. See docs/adr/0008 (closing note, karr #67).
  - SECURITY: the random data key and the per-value GCM nonce are now
    checked for length where they are generated, and there is only one
    generator left. _random_bytes existed twice, in File::SOPS and in
    File::SOPS::Encrypted, and both ended in an unchecked read from
    /dev/urandom - which can return short, and returns undef on error.
    Nothing downstream could catch that: a data key short to 16 or 24
    bytes is a valid AES-128/AES-192 key, so encryption succeeded, this
    library decrypted it, and the sops CLI accepted the document and
    exited 0; a nonce short to anything from 1 to 31 bytes was likewise
    accepted at both ends, and a 1-byte nonce repeats under one data key
    within a few hundred values. A wrong length now dies naming the
    short return instead of reaching a key or a nonce. The /dev/urandom
    fallback is gone rather than fixed: it stood behind a silent eval on
    Crypt::PRNG, which ships in CryptX alongside the Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM
    this distribution loads at compile time, so it could not be reached
    by any install able to load the code - and had it been reached it
    would have downgraded a seeded CSPRNG without telling anyone. No
    correctly generated document is affected and no wire bytes move.
  - Fix Perl-encrypted YAML being rejected outright by the sops CLI:
    lastmodified was emitted as a bare RFC3339 scalar, which Go's YAML
    parser types as time.Time where sops expects a string, so sops
    refused the file before decrypting anything. It is now emitted
    quoted, as sops itself writes it. JSON was never affected. This
    means no YAML file written by 0.001 or 0.002 could be read by the
    sops CLI; such files are readable again after re-encrypting with
    this version.
  - Fix false booleans being written as unencrypted empty values:
    JSON::PP::Boolean overloads eq, so JSON->false eq '' is true and
    the empty-value check swallowed them. Affected documents also
    failed their own MAC verification on read-back.
  - Fix the string 'false' being encrypted as True, caused by a bare
    Perl truthiness fallback in both boolean serializers.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - a value's type now comes from the value, not from
    a pattern match on its text. sops types a value by what the YAML or
    JSON parser returned, so a quoted "false", "true", "1", "0", "007"
    or "1.50" is type:str while a bare false is type:bool; File::SOPS
    matched the text instead, so it returned the string "true" as a
    boolean and "007" as the integer 7, silently changing values it
    exists to preserve. In the other direction it wrote "007" as
    type:int and "1.50" as type:float with the source spelling as the
    plaintext, and since Go recomputes the MAC from the canonical form
    (7 and 1.5) the sops CLI refused such files outright. What changes
    for callers: a Perl string that reads like a number or a boolean is
    now written as type:str and comes back as that string, and a Perl
    number is written in Go's canonical form (1.50 as 1.5, 1e20 as
    100000000000000000000). type:bool now requires JSON->true /
    JSON->false or a true/false loaded from YAML or JSON, because Perl
    has no native boolean. Existing encrypted documents are unaffected;
    only what this release writes, and what it gives back, moves. The
    reasoning, the measurements behind it and the one case where Perl
    can be misled are recorded in docs/adr/0002.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - values are UTF-8 encoded unconditionally, like
    the AAD, instead of only when the scalar carries Perl's UTF-8 flag.
    Below U+0100 that flag is storage and not meaning - "caf\x{e9}" may
    be held as one byte or as two and Perl considers both the same
    string - but both serializers write it to the file as UTF-8 either
    way. So a value that reached the wire as Latin-1 disagreed with our
    own emitter: with unencrypted_suffix, on by default, the document
    failed its own MAC and sops reported "MAC mismatch"; when the value
    was encrypted the document was self-consistent but sops -d handed
    the value back as "!!binary Y2Fm6Q==" instead of "café". What
    changes for callers: passing UTF-8 BYTES to encrypt now
    double-encodes them, so decode at your own boundary first. That
    appeared to work before only for encrypted values - the emitter was
    already double-encoding such a caller's unencrypted ones, and those
    documents already failed verification. type:bytes is exempt on both
    sides and is how a caller says a scalar really is bytes. Recorded in
    docs/adr/0003.
  - New File::SOPS::Encrypted class methods detect_type and
    value_to_bytes, the single source of truth for the value-to-wire
    mapping. The type ladder and the value conversion previously existed
    twice, once for the ciphertext and once for the MAC digest, and had
    to be kept byte-identical by hand - when they drifted both were
    consistently wrong and only the sops binary noticed.
  - Fix compilation when JSON::PP has not been loaded by something else
    first: _deserialize_value used JSON::PP::true/false as barewords
    behind a runtime require, so File::SOPS failed to compile on its own
    (JSON::MaybeXS does not pull in JSON::PP when Cpanel::JSON::XS is
    installed). Now uses JSON->true/JSON->false via JSON::MaybeXS.
  - Guard against dying on unblessed references reaching the value
    serializers, and route all boolean handling through JSON::MaybeXS.
  - SECURITY: decrypt no longer returns data it could not verify. A
    missing mac, a mac that is not a well-formed ENC value, and a mac
    that will not decrypt under the data key and the document's
    lastmodified all silently disabled verification and returned the
    document as though it had been checked. All three now die, matching
    sops, which reports "File has no MAC" / "Cannot decrypt MAC" and
    stops. Pass ignore_mac => 1 to decrypt, decrypt_file, extract or
    rotate for the old behaviour; what it returns is decrypted but not
    authenticated.
  - Fix MAC verification of any document containing a value excluded
    from encryption. sops covers unencrypted values in the MAC on both
    sides; File::SOPS hashed them when encrypting and skipped them when
    decrypting, so with the default unencrypted_suffix of _unencrypted
    it rejected its own output and could not read any sops file with
    such a key. No configuration was needed to hit it.
  - Fix MAC verification of values Perl's numeric conversion does not
    round-trip. The decrypt side hashed the value after int() / + 0.0,
    so '007' was hashed as 007 when written and 7 when read, and the
    expanded 100000000000000000000 that sops writes for 1e20 came back
    as 1e+20. It now hashes the authenticated plaintext.
  - Fix decrypt being non-deterministic on identical input. A type:float
    value came back an order of magnitude out on roughly one leaf in a
    hundred, while the ciphertext, the MAC and the document were all
    intact - which is why this looked like a test flake for two releases
    rather than the wrong-value bug it was. The cause is upstream:
    CryptX (measured on 0.087) returns the decrypted plaintext in a
    scalar whose string buffer is not NUL-terminated, and Perl's
    string-to-number conversion reads that buffer as a C string for
    every float and for every integer wider than 64 bits. So + 0.0 ran
    off the end of the plaintext into whatever byte the allocator had
    left there, and when that byte was one of the ten digit characters
    it joined the number: 100000000000000000000 read as 1e21. The
    plaintext is now normalised where it crosses out of CryptX, in
    decrypt_bytes, so the type conversion, the MAC digest and any caller
    doing arithmetic on what that method returns are all covered.
    t/18-decrypt-determinism.t pins it.
  - Fix MAC verification of documents with a key ending in "mac". The
    skip that kept the metadata MAC out of its own digest matched any
    key containing "mac:", so hmac and webmac values were dropped from
    the digest. The metadata MAC is now excluded structurally, by
    dropping the sops branch, and the pattern match is gone.
  - Honour mac_only_encrypted, including the fixed initialization block
    sops uses to keep the two settings' digests apart, and accept it as
    an option to encrypt / encrypt_file. rotate carries it over.
  - New File::SOPS::Encrypted method decrypt_bytes, returning the
    authenticated plaintext without type conversion, and new
    File::SOPS::Metadata method should_encrypt_path.
  - MAC verification now recovers document key order from an
    order-preserving reparse (YAML::PP, new prerequisite) instead of
    scraping ENC values out of the raw text, which is what makes
    unencrypted values placeable at all. YAML::XS and JSON::MaybeXS
    remain the parsers and emitters.
  - Fix any document with a non-ASCII key. The AAD is built from the key
    path and was handed to AES-GCM as a character string, so a key above
    U+00FF died with "Wide character in subroutine entry" and a key in
    the Latin-1 range was quietly authenticated as Latin-1 where sops
    used UTF-8 - a file that looks correct and that neither sops nor
    this library can read back. The AAD is now always UTF-8, matching
    both sops and the bytes our own emitters write the key as. Affects
    reading and writing, and the MAC, which derives the same AAD.
  - Fix decrypted values coming back as UTF-8 bytes where encrypt took
    characters. A decrypted structure compared unequal to the one that
    was encrypted, and decrypt_file encoded those bytes a second time,
    so a non-ASCII file round-tripped through encrypt_file/decrypt_file
    came back as mojibake. decrypt, decrypt_file and extract now return
    characters. The bytes written to the file are unchanged. See the new
    "Character encoding" section in File::SOPS for the boundary rule.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - the top-level 'sops' key is reserved, and both
    ways of colliding with it now die instead of succeeding silently.
    encrypt refuses a data structure carrying that key, because
    serialization writes the metadata there and the user's value was
    simply overwritten - after the digest had already covered it, so the
    document failed its own MAC on the next read. encrypt_file refuses
    an input that already has a sops section, because parsing split it
    off before encrypt ever saw it and every ENC[...] string was then
    encrypted a second time under a NEW data key, while the section
    holding the key they were encrypted with was discarded rather than
    written back - so the values became unrecoverable, and with output
    defaulting to input that happened over the original. Measured: sops
    makes no distinction between
    the two either, and refuses both with exit code 203 and the same
    message. Use rotate to re-key an encrypted file. The format
    handlers' serialize method refuses the same collision.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - the same rule now covers a top-level 'sops' entry
    that is not a mapping, which was still being deleted silently. A
    PLAINTEXT file containing 'sops: mine', a list, or an explicit null
    lost that key: File::SOPS::Metadata::from_hash returned undef for
    anything that was not a HashRef, and the format handlers call it
    only after seeing the key exist - so the entry was removed from the
    tree and then reported as no metadata section at all, which is what
    the guard above tests for. encrypt_file wrote the rest of the
    document without the key and said nothing, over the original where
    output defaulted to input; decrypt reported the generic "No SOPS
    metadata found" for a section it had just discarded. Both now die,
    naming the shape the entry had. Measured: sops refuses such a file
    whatever the entry holds - exit code 203 on encrypt, the same
    reserved-key message as for an already-encrypted file, and "Found
    sops entry that is not a mapping" on decrypt and rotate. What
    changes for direct callers of from_hash: it no longer returns undef
    for a non-HashRef, it dies. undef dies with the rest, because
    delete $data->{sops} cannot tell an absent entry from one holding
    null and sops refuses the second; a document with no sops key never
    reaches from_hash and still parses to no metadata.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - an integer outside Go's int64 range is refused
    rather than written. Perl's integers reach 2**64-1 and Go's int
    stops at 2**63-1, and there is no wire form that preserves the
    difference: written as type:int, sops -d stops with "strconv.Atoi:
    value out of range" (exit 25); unencrypted, it stopped the YAML walk
    outright ("unknown type: uint64") or produced a MAC mismatch in
    JSON, because Go re-derived the digest from the float64 it parsed.
    sops's own JSON store silently truncates such an integer to a
    float64 and its YAML store refuses the document; this release
    refuses in both, and says so at the key path. Store the digits as a
    STRING to keep them - that is type:str, written verbatim, and it
    survives both implementations. Reading is deliberately unaffected
    for values sops legitimately writes: its truncated 12345678901234567000
    parses back as a Perl integer above int64 and must still hash as
    those digits. Reading a type:int whose plaintext is out of range, or
    is not an integer at all, now dies as Go does; decrypt_bytes still
    returns the plaintext, which is how such a value is recovered.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - an unknown type: field is now an error ("Unknown
    datatype", as Go says) instead of quietly returning the raw
    plaintext, so a document this library cannot interpret no longer
    looks like it was read. type:time and type:comment are added to the
    ladder rather than rejected: sops emits both - a bare RFC3339 scalar
    or date becomes type:time, and a YAML comment becomes a type:comment
    node written as #ENC[...] on its own line - and both already worked
    by accident through the unknown branch. A type:time value is
    returned as its RFC3339 string, since Perl has no native date. A
    type:float plaintext that is not a number is an error too, where
    + 0.0 turned it into 0 without a word.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - base64 in an ENC value is now decoded strictly.
    MIME::Base64 ignores characters outside the alphabet and stops at a
    short group, so a corrupted data, iv or tag field decoded to
    something shorter and surfaced much later as "Authentication failed
    - data may be corrupted", or as a cipher rejecting the nonce length.
    Go fails immediately, and names the field. is_encrypted is
    unaffected and still never dies: it answers about the shape.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - encrypt_value refuses an empty value. GCM
    ciphertext is as long as its plaintext, so an empty one produced
    ENC[AES256_GCM,data:,...] - a string neither this module's own regex
    nor sops accepts ("Input string ENC[...] does not match sops' data
    format", exit 25). sops leaves empty values unencrypted, which is
    what File::SOPS::encrypt does before it ever calls encrypt_value.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - extract dies on a path that does not exist,
    naming the component that was not found. A missing TOP-LEVEL key
    used to return undef while a missing nested one died, so the same
    mistake was silent or loud depending on where it was made - and
    undef was indistinguishable from a key whose value really is null.
    sops reports "component ['nope'] not found" at every level.
  - Fix extract's bracket notation with an array index. The parser only
    recognised double-quoted components, so the documented ["items"][0]
    matched "items" alone and returned the whole ArrayRef instead of the
    element. Bare indices and single-quoted keys now work, as they do in
    sops --extract. Its POD claimed extract was more efficient than
    decrypting the whole file (it decrypts the whole file) and that it
    returned a scalar and not a reference (extracting a branch returns
    the branch, as sops does); both claims are corrected.
  - Fix undef being written as an empty string. sops leaves a null alone
    in YAML and in JSON alike, so a key whose value was null came back
    from File::SOPS as '' and reached sops as '' too. Both hash as
    nothing, so the digest never noticed and the document verified
    either way - the value was simply not the one that went in.
  - Fix $YAML::XS::Boolean being set at load time. It is a process
    global that changes what YAML::XS::Load and ::Dump do for every
    other user of them in the same interpreter, so merely loading
    File::SOPS rewrote how unrelated code parsed YAML. It is now set
    with local around this distribution's own calls. decrypt_file, which
    calls YAML::XS::Dump directly, was depending on that global to avoid
    emitting booleans as !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean, and now sets
    it for itself.
  - Failures say where and why. A value that would not parse, decrypt or
    place was skipped during the MAC walk, so the digest quietly covered
    a different document than the one on disk and the only symptom was
    "MAC verification failed" with no indication of the culprit - which
    is what made every other MAC defect in this distribution expensive
    to find. Value errors now name the key path on both the encrypt and
    the decrypt side, a structural disagreement between the document and
    the parsed tree is reported at the path it happens at instead of
    truncating the digest, and the MAC message says how many leaves were
    covered, in which order, and how to read the file anyway. No error
    message contains a plaintext value, a data key or an age identity -
    the key path only, which a SOPS document leaves readable by design.
    The digests themselves are deliberately not printed, unlike sops:
    a SHA-512 over the concatenated plaintexts is an oracle for a
    document with one short secret in it.
  - Fix multi-document YAML being silently reduced to its last document.
    The stream was loaded in scalar context, which returns only that one,
    so encrypting a two-document file wrote a single document back and
    discarded the rest without an error - data loss on a write path, and
    unrecoverable when encrypting in place. Such input is now refused.
    sops does support multi-document YAML, but as one tree of several
    branches: one metadata section copied into every document and one MAC
    spanning all of them in order. Matching that is a data-model change
    and is not in this release, so the file is rejected rather than
    truncated, and reading a multi-document sops file now says so instead
    of reporting a MAC mismatch. The measured behaviour of the reference
    implementation is recorded under "Multi-document YAML" in
    File::SOPS::Format::YAML. The MAC's order-preserving reparse now holds
    the same one-document rule on its own: YAML::PP and YAML::XS pick
    opposite ends of a multi-document stream in scalar context, so a
    reparse that accepted one would have taken its key order from a
    different document than its values.
  - Document how a caller says what a value is, in the new "Saying what
    a value is" section of File::SOPS. A scalar read in numeric context
    is retyped in place, so `if ($cfg->{port} > 1024)` before
    encrypting writes type:int; reading a scalar numerically leaves its
    string intact, so $cfg->{port} = "$cfg->{port}" restores both the
    type and the original text, and 0 + $x / 0.0 + $x ask for the other
    direction. No per-leaf type argument was added to encrypt: the
    scalar is the channel, and the one case the idiom cannot fix (a
    numeric ASSIGNMENT, which replaces the string before this module
    sees it) is one no argument could fix either.
  - Fix the encryption rules being applied one level at a time instead
    of to a value's whole key path. sops encrypts a leaf as soon as
    SOME component of its path matches, and the tree walk asked about
    each key as it descended - right for unencrypted_suffix and
    unencrypted_regex, where an excluded branch stays excluded anyway,
    and wrong for encrypted_suffix and encrypted_regex: everything
    under an `api_enc:` block was left readable, and a `token_enc:`
    under an ordinary parent was never reached because the walk stopped
    descending there. The resulting file was one sops refused. Latent
    until now only because no rule could be configured at all.
  - SECURITY: rotate no longer silently discards the parts of the sops
    section it did not create. It re-encrypted through encrypt, which
    built fresh metadata with the defaults, so a rotated file lost its
    encryption rules and every backend's key material except age -
    which survived only because rotate re-read the age recipients by
    hand. Rotating a file shared with PGP or KMS recipients therefore
    revoked their access while reporting success, and the file kept
    decrypting perfectly for whoever ran the command, so nothing looked
    wrong until someone else needed it. rotate now carries the
    document's rules, mac_only_encrypted and unmodelled fields across,
    and REFUSES a file holding key material it cannot re-encrypt (pgp,
    kms, gcp_kms, azure_kv, hc_vault, key_groups) rather than dropping
    it: a new data key makes those entries stale whatever is done with
    them. Rotate such a file with the sops CLI, or drop those
    recipients deliberately with decrypt followed by encrypt.
  - rotate also dies with a clear message on a file that has no sops
    section, instead of on an undefined method call.
  - New File::SOPS::Metadata method key_material_fields.
  - The encryption rules are reachable at last. unencrypted_suffix,
    encrypted_suffix, unencrypted_regex and encrypted_regex have been
    documented since 0.001 and could not be set through the public API:
    encrypt always built its metadata with the defaults, so the only
    rule that could ever be in effect was the _unencrypted default.
    All four are now arguments to encrypt and encrypt_file, and a whole
    policy can be carried over from an existing document with
    metadata => $meta. Pass unencrypted_suffix => undef for a document
    with no rule at all.
  - The four rules are mutually exclusive, and setting two now dies
    here instead of producing a file sops refuses to open ("Cannot use
    more than one of encrypted_suffix, unencrypted_suffix, ..."). The
    _unencrypted default stands down as soon as another rule is set,
    which is what sops does when creating a document.
  - Metadata no longer drops the fields of the sops section it does not
    model. shamir_threshold, key_groups and anything a later sops adds
    are preserved verbatim across a rewrite, in the new extra
    attribute. sops preserves the fields it knows and drops the rest;
    preserving everything is a superset of that and a safe one, since
    it ignores what it does not recognise.
  - New File::SOPS::Metadata attribute extra and methods policy_args
    and rule_value.
  - New method encrypt_in_place, documented in the design notes since
    0.001 and never implemented. It is encrypt_file over a single file,
    said in one argument instead of two, so the input/output pair
    cannot be got wrong and it takes the same shape as edit and rotate.
    The write is ATOMIC, as it now is everywhere (see below).
    Permissions are preserved and a symlink is followed; hard links to
    the file are not, since the inode changes. An already-encrypted
    file is refused exactly as encrypt_file refuses it.
  - New method edit: decrypt, open $EDITOR, re-encrypt. The decrypted
    document goes to a 0600 file in a 0700 temporary directory of its
    own, as sops does, and is removed on every way out - returning,
    dying, or being signalled (SIGTERM/SIGHUP are caught, the directory
    removed, and the signal re-raised; Ctrl-C reaches the editor
    instead, because perl's system() ignores SIGINT while a child
    runs). Returns 0 without touching the file when the editor changed
    nothing, and dies with the file untouched when the editor fails,
    the result does not parse, or it carries a top-level sops entry of
    its own. Two deliberate differences from sops edit: there is no
    fallback to vim/nano/vi when EDITOR is unset (a library that opens
    an interactive editor unasked hangs an unattended script), and the
    document is re-encrypted under a NEW data key rather than the
    existing one - so edit refuses the same files rotate refuses, and
    for the same reason.
  - New method creation_rules_for: reads the .sops.yaml governing a
    file and returns the encrypt arguments its first matching creation
    rule asks for - recipients, plus whichever encryption rule and
    mac_only_encrypted that rule carries - so that
    `encrypt_in_place(file => $f, File::SOPS->creation_rules_for(file =>
    $f))` encrypts a file the way the repository holding it has decided
    its files are encrypted. Documented in the design notes since 0.001
    and never implemented; until now recipients always had to be passed
    explicitly and nothing here read a config file at all.
    path_regex is matched the way sops matches it (measured on 3.13.3):
    against the file made absolute and normalised and then taken
    relative to the directory holding the config file - not against the
    path as typed and not against the absolute path - so the same file
    takes the same rule whichever directory the caller is in and
    however it named it. Symlinks are not resolved, the first matching
    rule wins, a rule without path_regex is a catch-all, and age takes
    a comma-separated string or a YAML list.
    ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE: the search for .sops.yaml walks up from
    the FILE, where sops walks up from the current working directory.
    sops finds no config at all for an absolute path when it is run
    from elsewhere, and skips a .sops.yaml that sits between its
    working directory and the file; neither is a sensible contract for
    a library, whose caller's working directory has nothing to do with
    the file it was handed. The two agree in the ordinary case of one
    .sops.yaml at the top of a repository. $SOPS_CONFIG is likewise not
    read from the environment - pass config => $ENV{SOPS_CONFIG} to opt
    in - because a variable that redirects which public keys a secret
    is encrypted to should not be obeyed on behalf of a caller who
    never asked.
    A rule naming key material this distribution cannot produce (pgp,
    kms, gcp_kms, azure_keyvault, hc_vault_transit_uri, key_groups,
    shamir_threshold) is REFUSED rather than half-applied, as rotate
    refuses such a document: sops wraps the data key for every backend
    a rule names, so honouring the age half alone would write a file
    the config says several parties can read and only some can, and
    report success. So are a rule carrying two encryption rules, a
    comment-based rule, a matching rule with no age recipient, no
    config file, and a config where no rule matches - all of which sops
    exits non-zero on too.
  - edit's two refusals for what comes back from the editor stay apart:
    a top-level 'sops' entry of the caller's own is reported as the
    reserved key it is, and text that will not parse as a parse failure.
    The reserved-key message was reachable only for an entry that was a
    MAPPING - every other shape is refused inside the format handler's
    parse, and edit wraps that parse in an eval, so 'sops: mine' was
    reported as "The edited document does not parse" for a document that
    parses perfectly well. Never released: the parse-time refusal that
    made it reachable is new in this same release, and the file was left
    untouched either way. sops draws the line in the same place and does
    not care about the shape either - "Tree not valid for encryption"
    with the reserved-key text for a scalar, a list, a null and a
    mapping alike, against "Could not load tree, probably due to invalid
    syntax" for text that does not parse (measured on 3.13.3).
  - DATA LOSS - encrypt_file, decrypt_file and rotate now write their
    output atomically, as encrypt_in_place and edit already did. All
    three opened the target with '>', which truncates it before the
    first byte is written, and then checked neither the print nor the
    close - so a write that could not complete left an EMPTY file and
    still returned true. encrypt_file defaults output to input and
    rotate always writes back over the file it read, so what was
    emptied was the only copy; for rotate it was a file whose data key
    had already been replaced in memory, which no identity can recover.
    The document now goes to a temporary file next to the target and is
    renamed over it, so a write that cannot finish leaves the previous
    file exactly as it was, and a write that fails is reported instead
    of swallowed.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE that follows from it - a file written by any of
    those methods comes back with a NEW INODE, so hard links to it keep
    the old content. sops keeps the inode and updates every hard link;
    this trades that for not destroying the file. Nothing else moves:
    an existing target keeps its mode, a target that has to be created
    still gets 0666 against the umask, and a symlink is still resolved
    to its target - as sops does in all three cases. An output that is
    not a regular file (/dev/null, /dev/stdout, a fifo) is written
    through directly and keeps working. Documented under "How a file is
    written" in File::SOPS.
  - Replacing a file needs write permission on its DIRECTORY rather than
    on the file, and now refuses a read-only file in a writable
    directory with the same wording sops -e -i uses ("Could not open
    in-place file for writing: ...: permission denied"), where the
    atomic write introduced by the change above would otherwise have
    replaced it (rename() checks the directory, not the file). The
    atomic write is the only reason this became a thing: the old open
    '>' would have failed on the chmod for free, and the early
    releases of this distribution maintained that refusal by accident
    through encrypt_in_place and edit. Every method that writes a file
    through _replace_file now enforces it. See karr #46.
  - The message for an already-encrypted input now points at edit as
    well as rotate, as sops's own exit-203 message points at its editor
    mode.
  - New method emit on File::SOPS::Format::YAML and
    File::SOPS::Format::JSON, emitting a document with no sops section -
    and decrypt_file and edit now write their plaintext through it
    instead of through an emitter of their own. That copy had to stay
    byte-identical to the handlers' and nothing checked that it did: for
    JSON it was a second JSON::MaybeXS encoder with utf8, pretty and
    canonical typed out again, and for YAML a dump that wrote `true`
    rather than `!!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 1` only because loading
    File::SOPS::Format::YAML assigned $YAML::XS::Boolean process-wide.
    Neither option is cosmetic - canonical is what makes the emitted key
    order sorted, and the MAC's encrypt side hashes in sorted order - so
    a drift between the two would have shown up as a document failing
    its own digest. Nothing about the output moves: emit is the code
    serialize was already dumping through, and both formats write the
    same bytes as before, plaintext and encrypted alike.
    t/20-plaintext-emitter.t pins that there is one emitter and not two
    that happen to agree: it replaces emit and requires decrypt_file,
    edit and serialize to follow it.
  - Fix JSON output and JSON parsing depending on which backend the
    CALLING program happened to load. Both went through JSON::MaybeXS,
    which binds once per process to whichever of Cpanel::JSON::XS and
    JSON::XS is already loaded, so the same data was written two ways by
    the same library - and CryptX, which this distribution loads for
    AES-GCM, pulls JSON::XS in on its own. File::SOPS::Format::JSON now
    names Cpanel::JSON::XS, for the emitter and the parser, which adds
    it as a direct prerequisite (4.38, the version JSON::MaybeXS itself
    asks for, so it is usually already installed). Two measured defects
    go with it, both of them only reachable in a process that had
    JSON::XS loaded first:
      * A sops-written JSON document holding an UNENCRYPTED float was
        refused with "MAC verification failed". JSON::XS's decoder is
        not correctly rounded - it reads 0.3 back as the double whose
        shortest form is 0.30000000000000004 - so the digest computed
        here covered a different number than the one in the file. The
        MAC is what caught it; passing ignore_mac would have returned
        the wrong value.
      * An unencrypted -0.0 was written as `-0`, which parses back as
        the integer zero and digests as "0" while the MAC covers "-0".
        Such a document fails its own MAC here and in sops (exit 51).
        sops writes `-0` itself and cannot read that file back either.
    WHICH DOCUMENTS LOOK DIFFERENT: JSON written from a process that
    loaded JSON::XS first, floats only - an unencrypted 1.0 is now
    written as 1.0 rather than 1, and -0.0 as -0.0 rather than -0.
    Integers, strings, booleans, nulls, key order and unicode are
    unchanged, encrypted values are ENC[...] strings and were never
    affected, and the YAML path has no backend choice and does not move
    at all. A process that loads File::SOPS the ordinary way already got
    these bytes; for it nothing changes.
    OLD DOCUMENTS STAY READABLE, with one exception that never was: a
    `-0` written for a float under unencrypted_suffix failed its own MAC
    the moment it was written, here and in sops. Re-encrypt such a
    document from its plaintext; nothing else needs touching.
    JSON parsing is also stricter in one respect, now identically in
    every process: a document with DUPLICATE KEYS is refused rather than
    silently resolved to the last value, which is what JSON::XS did.
    Such a document cannot have a well-defined MAC. The decision, the
    measurements behind it and why matching sops's own float rendering
    would have been the wrong direction are recorded in docs/adr/0005.
  - Tests: the interop suite is no longer the only compatibility
    coverage. New regression tests pin the wire format, the serializers'
    key ordering, every MAC rule above and the encoding boundary without
    requiring a sops binary, which is what let the above ship unnoticed.
    The encoding tests include a document written by the real sops
    3.13.3, checked in with its throwaway age identity, so the read side
    is pinned against the reference implementation even when the binary
    is absent. Four more files pin the reserved sops key, the integer
    range, the API edges above and the diagnostics, and the interop
    suite gained the Go half of each: the exit-203 refusal, sops's own
    int64 boundary in both formats, nulls in both directions, and a real
    sops document containing timestamps and comments. encrypt_in_place
    and edit have a file of their own covering what each does when it
    goes wrong - the plaintext left on disk, the file left half
    written - and both check the result against sops -d. A further file
    pins the atomic write for encrypt_file, decrypt_file and rotate by
    making the write itself fail under RLIMIT_FSIZE 0 and looking at
    what is left, which is the only way to tell the fix from the bug:
    both leave a file behind.
  - BUG FIX: a document that contains itself is now refused, naming the path
    at which the cycle closes, where it used to HANG the process. YAML::XS
    resolves a recursive anchor (`root: &a` / `  b: *a`) into a real Perl
    cycle and hands it over, so all eight public entry points walked it
    forever and never came back: encrypt, encrypt_file and encrypt_in_place
    in the MAC's leaf collection, decrypt, decrypt_file, extract, rotate and
    edit in the decrypt walk. Not a croak a caller could catch and not a
    truncated result - no return at all, and a memory climb with it, which
    for a document that arrived from somewhere the caller does not control
    is resource exhaustion by a file. A caller who hands `encrypt` a
    self-referential Perl structure of their own reached the same hang by a
    different road and is refused by the same guard, with a message that
    names both origins. Refusing is what the reference implementation does:
    measured against sops 3.13.3, the same document is rejected in both
    directions - `Error unmarshalling file: yaml: anchor 'a' value contains
    itself` (exit 2) from sops -e, and `yaml: anchor 'a' value contains
    itself` (exit 1) from sops -d - and the refusal comes AHEAD of the key,
    since that document with no usable age identity still reports the cycle
    rather than a failure to get the data key, so this one does too.
    Terminating the walks with a visited set instead was rejected as the
    worse answer: it would have written a file with the alias expanded once
    and a MAC over a tree that is not the one the caller gave, quietly.
    AN ANCHOR THAT IS MERELY REUSED IS UNAFFECTED and was never the problem
    - sharing a subtree is ordinary YAML, sops accepts it and expands it to
    independent ENC values, and so does this; only a container that is its
    own ancestor is refused, so a diamond of 2**40 paths still passes, in
    0.3ms. Nothing is written on the way out either: encrypt_in_place leaves
    the original untouched and encrypt_file creates no output. Still open as
    karr #112, and deliberately not fixed here: an ACYCLIC document that
    shares aliases exponentially hangs the same walks, which sops refuses
    separately with `yaml: document contains excessive aliasing`. See
    docs/adr/0025 and karr #110.
  - BUG FIX: a YAML document that sops writes with a bare `.inf`, `-.inf` or
    `.nan` in an unencrypted slot is now readable, where it used to fail MAC
    verification with an error that named nothing. libyaml leaves those
    spellings a STRING; gopkg.in/yaml.v3 resolves each to a float and sops
    digests `+Inf` / `-Inf` / `NaN`, so our digest covered four characters and
    Go's covered four different ones. Measured against sops 3.13.3, twelve
    tokens in an unencrypted slot: 0 of 12 read before, 12 of 12 after, with
    `sops -e` and `sops -d` at exit 0 on every one of them throughout.
    decrypt_file now reproduces sops's own plaintext for such a document, byte
    for byte. WHAT MADE THIS DIFFERENT from every other repair in this
    distribution is that the missing fact is not in the scalar: a bare `.inf`
    and a quoted `".inf"` have DIFFERENT sops digests (`+Inf` against `.inf`)
    and YAML::XS hands back the same string for both - measured in one
    document, sops writing `- .inf` and `- ".inf"` as adjacent list elements
    and reading both back at exit 0. So the repair asks YAML::PP, already this
    distribution's second parser, whether the document wrote the scalar PLAIN,
    and only then replaces it - with a dualvar carrying the float go-yaml
    resolves and the document's own token, so the value is right numerically
    and the emitter writes the file back exactly as it read it. The value
    still comes from the existing Go model and never from YAML::PP. NOTHING
    THAT WORKED STOPS WORKING: a quoted token, the twelve near-miss spellings
    (`.INf` `+.nan` `.infinity` `Inf` `NaN` and the rest), an encrypted
    `type:float` whose plaintext is `+Inf`, and ADR 0023's overflow literals
    are all byte-identical before and after. A plaintext document is not
    touched either, so encrypt_file still gives ADR 0013's refusal, which
    names the key path and both resolvers. STILL REFUSED, and deliberately:
    such a document cannot be written back - rotate and edit croak from the
    karr #59 non-finite guard, which is untouched, though it now names the
    leaf where a MAC error named nothing. That guard turns out to be too wide
    for this one scalar shape (measured: with it bypassed in a scratch
    process, the document written is `sops -d` exit 0), and narrowing it is
    karr #113 with its own corpus. Cost: a document that never mentions
    `.inf` or `.nan` in its raw bytes pays nothing measurable, 0.096ms per
    parse before and after; one that really holds a token pays a second
    YAML::PP load. See docs/adr/0026 and karr #105.
  - BUG FIX and NEW GUARD: a document nested deeper than 100 levels no
    longer floods STDERR while succeeding, and one nested deeper than sops
    can carry is refused instead of walked. Perl warns "Deep recursion on
    subroutine" past a fixed depth of 100, which is about perl and not about
    the document: measured on a 265-level alias chain sops accepts, a correct
    encrypt wrote 505 warning lines and 63 KB to STDERR. The walks now say
    `no warnings 'recursion'` - per walk, so nothing else loses the warning -
    and carry a bound of their own instead: $File::SOPS::MAX_DEPTH, 10000
    containers, refused in every walk and in both directions ahead of the
    data key. The number is sops's, measured against 3.13.3: go-yaml accepts
    10001 containers and refuses 10002 ("yaml: exceeded max depth of 10000"),
    Go's JSON encoder accepts 10000 and refuses 10001, so 10000 is the
    deepest a document can be and still be readable in both formats - one
    level tighter than go-yaml alone, deliberately, because the alternative
    is writing a JSON file sops cannot read back. The variable is writable:
    lower it to refuse deep documents earlier, raise it and sops will refuse
    what you write. Deep documents also got cheaper - the walks now push and
    pop one key path instead of copying it per level, which was quadratic in
    depth (4000 levels took 101s and 410 MB in the MAC's walk alone; 10000
    levels now take 0.017s and 24 MB), and the 265-level fixture encrypts in
    0.417s where it took 0.807s. Not fixed yet: the walks in Encrypted.pm and
    Format/YAML.pm still warn, 168 of the original 505 lines, karr #120. See
    docs/adr/0029 and karr #117.

  - NEW: a plaintext YAML tagging a scalar `!!bool true` or `!!bool false` is
    now encrypted instead of refused, and the other yaml.org scalar tags are
    refused with a message about the document instead of one about libyaml.
    YAML::XS accepts only !!str, !!int and !!float on a scalar and dies on the
    rest with `bad tag found for scalar`, which reads like a bug in a foreign
    library; sops accepts them all, resolves them and drops the tag, so a
    hand-written plaintext `sops -e` encrypts at exit 0 could not be opened
    here. Only one of those tags carries nothing: measured against sops
    3.13.3 with the stored MAC decrypted out of each document, `!!bool true`
    and a bare `true` produce byte-identical output - type:bool, plaintext
    True, same digest - so that tag is dropped before parsing, by the same
    count-reconciled retry the !!merge repair uses (docs/adr/0028) and only on
    a plain true/false. Every other tag carries a TYPE, and the type comes
    from the scalar here (docs/adr/0002), so removing it would retype the
    leaf: sops base64-DECODES !!binary (`aGVsbG8=` becomes the value `hello`),
    re-renders !!timestamp in RFC3339 under type:time (`2026-08-21` becomes
    `2026-08-21T00:00:00Z`), resolves !!bool on True/TRUE where libyaml leaves
    a string, and suppresses its own resolver for !!value/!!set/!!omap/!!seq/
    !!map. Those are refused by name, saying what sops resolves the tag to and
    naming the leaf's key path - never quoting the scalar's own text back,
    which is plaintext. Nothing that parsed before parses differently, and no
    AAD path, digest byte or emitted byte moves. Two divergences found while
    measuring and deliberately left open: `0755` is the integer 493 to sops
    and 755 here, tagged or not (karr #29's class; writing it unencrypted was
    already refused by the foreign-resolution guard), and `!!float 1` is
    type:float there and type:int here with the same plaintext, so the file
    still verifies. See docs/adr/0032 and karr #118.
  - DECISION, no behaviour change: the per-format type policy the ENV and INI
    handlers were waiting on is settled and written down as docs/adr/0035.
    Nothing a caller can invoke changes - there is still no ENV or INI handler
    (karr #36, #37) - but two POD sections now carry the rule instead of an
    open question. The ticket's premise turned out to be half wrong: `type:str`
    on everything is what an ENV or INI SOURCE produces, because its reader
    returns strings, and that falls out of docs/adr/0002 with no format rule at
    all; a typed source keeps `type:int`, `type:float`, `type:bool` and
    `type:time` in a dotenv or ini document in both implementations alike, and
    `sops -d` reads every one back at exit 0. What is really broken is the
    UNENCRYPTED slot, which carries no label, so the untyped reader hands the
    digest the literal text of the line. Measured over the whole ladder, both
    formats, both slots: the `sops_mac` plaintext is SHA-512 of
    File::SOPS::Encrypted->value_to_bytes on EVERY row - while sops's own
    emitter writes a display form for three classes and then cannot read the
    file it just wrote (`true` against a digest over `True`, karr #124; `<nil>`
    against a digest over the empty string, karr #125, exit 25 in an encrypted
    slot; and `1.0` / `1E+20` / `-0.0` against digests over `1` /
    `100000000000000000000` / `-0`, which was in no ticket). The decision is
    that our emitters write value_to_bytes verbatim there: the digest does not
    move, each line is one sops itself writes for the same text, and every one
    reads back at exit 0. docs/adr/0002 is EXTENDED, not changed - no rung of
    the type ladder moves and no wire byte moves for any caller. The metadata
    half of the same ticket is decided too: sops decodes its section weakly in
    EVERY format (a quoted `mac_only_encrypted: "false"` is the boolean false
    in nested YAML as well), so the coercion belongs in
    File::SOPS::Metadata->from_hash and not in Metadata::Flat->unflatten, which
    stays faithful to the untyped encoding. See docs/adr/0035 and karr #77.

0.002     2026-02-22 02:10:34Z
  - Fix bool deserialization: return JSON::PP::Boolean instead of plain 1/0
    (YAML::XS with $Boolean='JSON::PP' now correctly serializes true/false)

0.001     2026-01-11 04:00:35Z
  - Initial release
  - Pure Perl implementation of Mozilla SOPS encrypted file format
  - Full interoperability with sops CLI (tested with v3.9.4)
  - age encryption backend via Crypt::Age
  - AES-256-GCM value encryption with type preservation (str, int, float, bool)
  - YAML and JSON format support
  - MAC verification for tamper detection
  - Multiple recipient support
  - Key rotation support
  - File encrypt/decrypt operations
  - Single value extraction via path notation
  - Comprehensive test suite including CLI interop tests
